es the rabbit.\n11. The rabbit is round.\n12. The rabbit sees the bald eagle.\n13. If someone eats the bald eagle and they see the rabbit, then they are round.\n14. If someone eats the bald eagle and they eat the rabbit, then the rabbit likes the cat.\n15. If someone is young and they eat the rabbit, then the rabbit sees the cat.\n16. If someone eats the rabbit and the rabbit is cold, then they are blue.\n17. If the rabbit sees the cat and the rabbit is round, then the cat eats the rabbit.\n18. If the bald eagle sees the bear and the bald eagle likes the bear, then the bald eagle is red.\n19. If someone eats the rabbit and they are blue, then they eat the bald eagle.\n20. If someone likes the cat, then the cat is round.\n21. If the rabbit likes the cat and the cat is round, then the rabbit is blue.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the rabbit does not see the cat. So, I need to determine if the rabbit sees the cat or not.\n\nLooking at the given statements, I see that statement 12 says the rabbit sees the bald eagle. So, the rabbit sees the bald eagle, but does that mean it sees the cat as well? Not necessarily. The question is about seeing the cat, not the bald eagle.\n\nI should check if there\'s any information about the rabbit seeing the cat. Let\'s look through the statements again.\n\nStatement 17 says: If the rabbit sees the cat and the rabbit is round, then the cat eats the rabbit. But this is a conditional statement; it doesn\'t tell us whether the rabbit actually sees the cat or not.\n\nStatement 20: If someone likes the cat, then the cat is round. But again, this is a conditional and doesn\'t directly relate to the rabbit seeing the cat.\n\nLooking at statement 10: The cat eats the bald eagle. So, the cat is known to eat the bald eagle, but that doesn\'t necessarily mean the cat sees the rabbit or the bald eagle. Wait, statement 12 says the rabbit sees the bald eagle, so the rabbit is aware of the bald eagle, but does that mean the rabbit sees the cat