ald eagle visits the squirrel.\n\nBut we don\'t know who is young yet. Statement 20 is the only one that connects visiting the squirrel with being young, but as we saw, it leads to a contradiction because the lion is blue. So perhaps the only way to resolve this is to see if the squirrel being red and being visited by the lion affects the bald eagle\'s age.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel being red and being visited by the lion implies that the squirrel is young, but the squirrel is red, so according to statement 20, the visitor (lion) is young. But the lion is blue, so that\'s a problem. Therefore, perhaps the squirrel isn\'t being visited by the lion, but that contradicts statement 9.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m overcomplicating this. Let\'s try to see if the bald eagle is young based on other statements.\n\nStatement 18: If something is young, it visits the bald eagle. So if the bald eagle is young, then it would visit the squirrel (from statement 19). But we don\'t know if the bald eagle is young yet.\n\nAlternatively, if the bald eagle is young, it would visit the squirrel, which would then cause the squirrel to visit the lion (statement 19). But the squirrel is red, so according to statement 20, the visitor (lion) would be young. But the lion is blue, so that\'s a contradiction. Therefore, the only way this works is if the squirrel isn\'t being visited by the lion, but statement 9 says it is. So this is a paradox.\n\nWait, maybe the squirrel being red and being visited by the lion implies that the lion is young, but the lion is blue, so that\'s impossible. Therefore, perhaps the squirrel isn\'t red, but statement 13 says it is. So this is a contradiction, meaning the given statements are inconsistent, but that can\'t be the case because the question is based on the given context.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel being red and being visited by the lion means that the squirrel is young, but the squirrel is red, so according to statement 20, the visitor (lion) i