omeone is blue or green, they are white. But wait, from point 12, if someone is blue, they are green. So if someone is blue, they are both green and white? That seems contradictory because from point 1, Bob is green. But Bob is also mentioned as blue in point 14, but wait, point 14 says if Bob is smart and blue, then he\'s not green. But Bob is green from point 1, so he can\'t be blue. Hmm, maybe I\'m getting ahead of myself.\n\nLet me focus on Erin. We know Erin is blue. From point 13, blue people are white. So Erin is white. From point 16, all white people are smart. Therefore, Erin is smart.\n\nWait, but the question is asking if Erin is not smart. So if Erin is smart, then the statement "Erin is not smart" would be false. But let me double-check.\n\nWait, point 13 says "Blue, green people are white." So if someone is blue or green, they are white. But from point 12, if someone is blue, they are green. So being blue implies being green, and thus white. But Bob is green (point 1) and also, from point 14, if Bob is smart and blue, he\'s not green. But Bob is green, so he can\'t be blue. So Bob is green and not blue. Therefore, Bob is not blue, so he\'s not in the category of blue or green, so point 13 doesn\'t apply to him. But Erin is blue, so she is both blue and white. Therefore, Erin is white, and from point 16, all white people are smart. So Erin is smart. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
