e both green and white, which seems impossible unless the color is a combination, but the context doesn\'t mention that. Alternatively, maybe the statements are exclusive.\n\nWait, point 6 says Gary is red and white. So maybe being red and white is possible for some things. But for Charlie, if he\'s green and white, that might not be possible unless it\'s allowed. But the context doesn\'t specify that, so maybe it\'s an inconsistency.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Charlie can\'t be both red and green. So if Charlie is smart, he must be red, but from point 8, he must be green to be smart. That\'s a contradiction unless he\'s both, which isn\'t possible. So perhaps Charlie can\'t be smart because it leads to a contradiction.\n\nWait, but let\'s think again. If Charlie is smart, then he must be red (from point 11). But from point 8, only green, furry things are smart. So if Charlie is smart, he must be green. But if he\'s green, then from point 9, he\'s white. So Charlie would have to be both green and white, which isn\'t possible unless it\'s allowed. But the context doesn\'t say that, so maybe Charlie can\'t be both. Therefore, Charlie can\'t be smart because it would require him to be both red and green, which isn\'t possible.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Charlie isn\'t smart because being smart would require him to be both red and green, which isn\'t possible. So Charlie can\'t be smart.\n\nWait, but let\'s check if there\'s another way. If Charlie isn\'t smart, then from point 14, being cold and kind (which he is) would imply he\'s white. So if Charlie is white, then from point 9, he\'s green. Then, from point 8, he\'s smart. So if Charlie isn\'t smart, that would mean he\'s not white, but he\'s kind and cold, which would make him white, leading to a contradiction. So Charlie can\'t be not smart because that would make him white, which would make him green, which would make him smart, which is a contradiction. Therefore, Charlie must be smart.\n\nWait, I\'m getting confused.