fact 14 says that kind, nice people are quiet. So if someone is kind and nice, they are quiet. But Bob is not kind, so does that affect his quietness? Not directly, because the statement is about kind and nice people being quiet, not the other way around.\n\nLooking at fact 15: rough people are nice. So if someone is rough, they are nice. Gary is rough, so he\'s nice, which we already know.\n\nFact 16: If someone is white, they are rough. So white people are rough. But Erin is white, so she\'s rough. That\'s consistent with fact 13, which says white, quiet people are blue. Since Erin is white and quiet, she must be blue, which aligns with fact 6 (Erin is white) and fact 13.\n\nNow, going back to Bob. Bob is not kind. Let\'s see if there\'s any rule that connects not being kind to being quiet or not. Fact 14 says kind and nice people are quiet. But Bob isn\'t kind, so he might or might not be quiet. There\'s no direct rule that says if someone isn\'t kind, they aren\'t quiet. It\'s possible for someone to be quiet without being kind, but it\'s also possible for them to not be quiet.\n\nWait, but let\'s think about the relationships. Fact 12 says nice people are kind. So if someone is nice, they are kind. But Bob isn\'t kind, so he can\'t be nice. Because if he were nice, he\'d have to be kind, but he isn\'t. So Bob isn\'t nice.\n\nNow, fact 15 says rough people are nice. So if someone is rough, they\'re nice. But Bob isn\'t rough because he\'s not kind, and from fact 14, kind, nice people are quiet. Wait, no, fact 14 is about kind and nice people being quiet, not about roughness.\n\nWait, let me clarify. Fact 14: Kind, nice people are quiet. So if someone is both kind and nice, they are quiet. But Bob isn\'t kind, so he doesn\'t fall into that category. However, fact 15 says rough people are nice. So if someone is rough, they\'re nice. But Bob isn\'t rough because he\'s not kind, and from fact 14, if he were nice, he\'d have to be kind, which he isn\'t. So Bob isn\'t