el (statement 4). So, both the cat and the squirrel eat the mouse, making them rough. Wait, but statement 1 says the cat is rough, which we already know. But does that mean the squirrel is also rough? Because the squirrel eats the mouse, so from statement 21, the squirrel is rough.\n\nNow, from statement 20: If something visits the rabbit and sees the squirrel, then the squirrel is rough. Since the squirrel is rough, does that mean that if the squirrel sees the rabbit, then the squirrel is rough? Wait, no, the statement is that if something (the squirrel) visits the rabbit and sees the squirrel (which is itself), then the squirrel is rough. But that\'s a bit confusing.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m overcomplicating. Let\'s try to see if the cat sees the squirrel.\n\nFrom statement 19: If the cat is rough and sees the cat (which it does, from statement 2), then the cat is blue. But the cat is already rough, but we don\'t know if it\'s blue yet. So, if the cat sees the squirrel, then the cat would be blue.\n\nBut from statement 17: If something is kind, it visits the rabbit. The mouse is kind (statement 5), so it visits the rabbit (statement 7). The cat sees the rabbit (statement 2), but does that make the cat kind? Not necessarily. The mouse is kind, but the cat is a different entity.\n\nWait, maybe the cat is kind because it sees the squirrel. From statement 13: If something sees the squirrel, then it is kind. So, if the cat sees the squirrel, then the cat is kind. Then, from statement 17, the cat would visit the rabbit. But the cat already visits the rabbit (statement 6). So, that\'s consistent.\n\nBut does the cat actually see the squirrel? I don\'t see any direct statement that the cat sees the squirrel. The only thing I know is that the mouse sees the squirrel (statement 4), and the mouse is kind (statement 5). So, the mouse sees the squirrel, which makes the mouse kind. But does that mean the squirrel is kind? Not necessarily, unless the squirrel sees something.\n\nWait,