 and from point 8: Nice → not green. So he can\'t be green. Therefore, Dave cannot be quiet because that would require him to be green, which he\'s not.\n\nOption 2: Dave is not quiet.\n\n- Then, from point 11: Nice → quiet, but Dave is not quiet, so that\'s a contradiction. Therefore, Dave cannot be not quiet because that would violate point 11.\n\nWait, that can\'t be right. If Dave is not quiet, then point 11 is violated because nice → quiet. Therefore, Dave must be quiet, but that leads to a contradiction. Therefore, the only consistent conclusion is that Dave is not quiet, which would mean that point 11 doesn\'t apply, but that can\'t be because point 11 is a rule.\n\nThis is confusing. Maybe the answer is that the statement is false because Dave must be quiet, but that leads to a contradiction, making the statement "Dave is not quiet" false.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the answer is that the statement is true because Dave cannot be quiet without leading to a contradiction, so the only possibility is that he is not quiet.\n\nWait, I think I\'m overcomplicating it. Let me try to see if there\'s a way for Dave to be not quiet without contradiction.\n\nIf Dave is not quiet, then from point 11: Nice → quiet, which would mean he\'s quiet, which contradicts him not being quiet. Therefore, Dave cannot be not quiet because that would make him quiet, which is a contradiction. Therefore, Dave must be quiet, making the statement "Dave is not quiet" false.\n\nWait, but if Dave is quiet, he must be blue (point 9), which makes him young (point 10), which makes him green (point 14), but he\'s nice, so he can\'t be green. Therefore, Dave cannot be quiet, which means the statement "Dave is not quiet" is true.\n\nI\'m going in circles. Let me try to think of it differently.\n\nFrom point 11: Nice → quiet. So Dave is quiet.\n\nFrom point 9: Quiet, blue things are big. So if Dave is quiet, he must be blue and big.\n\nFrom point 10: If something is blue, it\'s young. So Dave is young