messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nThe cow is young. The cow sees the lion. The dog likes the cow. The dog likes the rabbit. The dog sees the rabbit. The lion is young. The lion sees the cow. The lion sees the dog. The lion sees the rabbit. The rabbit is round. The rabbit likes the cow. If someone visits the rabbit then the rabbit likes the lion. If someone likes the rabbit and the rabbit is red then the rabbit is big. If someone likes the lion and they see the rabbit then the lion likes the rabbit. If someone is young then they like the rabbit. If someone is young then they see the lion. If the lion is big then the lion visits the dog. If the rabbit likes the lion then the rabbit is young. If someone is young and they like the rabbit then they visit the rabbit.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The lion sees the lion.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown. I think the correct option is: B\n', '<think>.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "The lion sees the lion" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context. Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the given statements to make it clearer:.\n\n1. The cow is young.\n2. The cow sees the lion.\n3. The dog likes the cow.\n4. The dog likes the rabbit.\n5. The dog sees the rabbit.\n6. The lion is young.\n7. The lion sees the cow.\n8. The lion sees the dog.\n9. The lion sees the rabbit.\n10. The rabbit is round.\n11. The rabbit likes the cow.\n12. If someone visits the rabbit, then the rabbit likes the lion.\n13. If someone likes the rabbit and the rabbit is red, then the rabbit is big.\n14. If someone likes the lion and they see the rabbit, then the lion likes the rabbit.\n15. If someone is young, then they like the rabbit.\n16. If someone is young, then they see the lion.\n17. If the lion is big, then the lion visits the dog.\n18. If the rabbit likes the lion, then the rabbit is young.\n19. If someone is young and they like the rabbit, then they visit the rabbit.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the lion sees the lion. So, I need to see if there\'s any information that directly or indirectly leads to this conclusion.\n\nLooking at the statements, I see that the lion is described as being young (statement 6). Statement 15 says that if someone is young, they like the rabbit. So, the lion, being young, likes the rabbit. Statement 18 then says that if the rabbit likes the lion, the rabbit is young. But wait, the rabbit is described as round (statement 10) and likes the cow (statement 11). There\'s no information that the rabbit is red, so statement 13 doesn\'t apply, meaning we don\'t know if the rabbit is big.\n\nNow, statement 14 says that if someone likes the lion and sees the rabbit, then the lion likes the rabbit. But the lion already likes the rabbit because the lion is young (statement 15). However, statement 14 is about someone liking the lion and seeing the rabbit leading to the lion liking the rabbit. It doesn\'t necessarily mean that the lion is seeing the lion.\n\nLooking at statement 17, if the lion is big, it visits the dog. But we don\'t know if the lion is big. Statement 18 says that if the rabbit likes the lion, the rabbit is young. But the rabbit is already young (statement 6), so that doesn\'t necessarily mean the rabbit likes the lion. Wait, no, statement 18 is an implication: if the rabbit likes the lion, then the rabbit is young. But since the rabbit is young, it doesn\'t necessarily mean that the rabbit likes the lion. It\'s possible that the rabbit likes the lion, but it\'s also possible that it doesn\'t. The statement is only one way.\n\nSo, putting it all together: The lion is young, likes the rabbit (statement 15), and the rabbit is young (statement 6). But there\'s no information that the lion is seeing the lion. The statements don\'t mention the lion interacting with itself. The only interactions are with the cow, dog, and rabbit. There\'s no rule that would lead to the lion seeing the lion.\n\nTherefore, based on the given information, we can\'t conclude that the lion sees the lion. It\'s possible, but not necessarily true. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 18%|#8        | 110/600 [48:33<27:39,  3.39s/it]
