messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nAnne is big. Anne is cold. Anne is furry. Anne is white. Erin is cold. Fiona is round. Gary is smart. All smart things are furry. Big, blue things are round. If Gary is cold then Gary is smart. All blue, furry things are big. If Gary is furry then Gary is blue. Round things are cold. Cold, white things are furry. Round things are blue.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Gary is big.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown. I think the correct option is: C\n', '<think>.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "Gary is big" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context. Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the facts provided:.\n\n1. Anne is big.\n2. Anne is cold.\n3. Anne is furry.\n4. Anne is white.\n5. Erin is cold.\n6. Fiona is round.\n7. Gary is smart.\n8. All smart things are furry.\n9. Big, blue things are round.\n10. If Gary is cold, then Gary is smart.\n11. Round things are cold.\n12. Cold, white things are furry.\n13. Round things are blue.\n\nNow, the question is about Gary being big. So I need to see if we can conclude that Gary is big based on these statements.\n\nLet\'s break it down:.\n\n- From statement 7, Gary is smart.\n- Statement 8 says all smart things are furry, so Gary is furry.\n- Statement 10 says if Gary is cold, then Gary is smart. But we already know Gary is smart, so this doesn\'t directly tell us if Gary is cold or not.\n- Statement 11 says round things are cold. So anything that\'s round must be cold.\n- Statement 13 says round things are blue. So if something is round, it\'s both cold and blue.\n- Statement 9 says big, blue things are round. So if something is big and blue, it\'s round.\n- Statement 12 says cold, white things are furry. Since Gary is smart and thus furry (from statement 8), but we don\'t know if he\'s cold or white.\n\nWait, let\'s see if we can link these together. We know Gary is smart, so he\'s furry (statement 8). Now, statement 12 says cold, white things are furry. Since Gary is furry, does that mean he\'s cold and white? Not necessarily. It\'s possible that Gary is furry for other reasons, but the statement only tells us that if something is cold and white, then it\'s furry. It doesn\'t say that all furry things are cold and white.\n\nSo, Gary is furry, but we don\'t know if he\'s cold or white. However, statement 11 says round things are cold. So if something is round, it\'s cold. Statement 9 says big, blue things are round. So if something is big and blue, it\'s round, which makes it cold.\n\nBut we don\'t know if Gary is blue. Statement 13 says round things are blue, but that doesn\'t mean all blue things are round. So Gary could be blue and round, making him big, but we don\'t have information about his color.\n\nWait, let\'s see. We know Gary is smart, so he\'s furry (statement 8). Statement 10: If Gary is cold, then he\'s smart. But we already know he\'s smart, so this doesn\'t help us determine if he\'s cold.\n\nStatement 11: Round things are cold. So if Gary is round, he\'s cold. But we don\'t know if he\'s round.\n\nStatement 9: Big, blue things are round. So if Gary is big and blue, he\'s round, which makes him cold. But again, we don\'t know if he\'s blue.\n\nSo, can we conclude that Gary is big? Let\'s see. We know Gary is smart, so he\'s furry. But to be big, he needs to be a big, blue thing to be round (from statement 9). But we don\'t know if Gary is blue. He could be blue, making him round and thus cold, but we don\'t have information about his color.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another way. Since Gary is smart, he\'s furry. From statement 12, cold, white things are furry. So if Gary is cold and white, he\'s furry. But we don\'t know if he\'s white. Anne is white, but that\'s a different person.\n\nWait, statement 12 says cold, white things are furry. So if Gary is cold and white, he\'s furry. But we already know he\'s furry because he\'s smart. So that doesn\'t help us determine if he\'s cold or white.\n\nI\'m getting a bit stuck here. Let me try to see if there\'s any other connection. Statement 13 says round things are blue. So if something is round, it\'s blue. But we don\'t know if Gary is round. Statement 9 says big, blue things are round. So if Gary is big and blue, he\'s round. But again, we don\'t know if he\'s blue.\n\nWait, maybe we can link it through being cold. If Gary is round (from being big and blue), then he\'s cold (from statement 11). But we don\'t know if he\'s big or blue.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can infer that since Gary is smart and thus furry, and from statement 12, cold, white things are furry, but we don\'t know if Gary is cold or white. So he could be either, but we don\'t have enough info to say for sure.\n\nWait, but statement 10 says if Gary is cold, then he\'s smart. But we already know he\'s smart, so that doesn\'t help us determine if he\'s cold.\n\nI think I\'m going in circles. Let me try to see if there\'s any way to conclude that Gary is big. From the given statements, the only way Gary can be big is if he\'s blue and big, making him round (from statement 9). But we don\'t know if he\'s blue. So we can\'t conclude that he\'s big.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another angle. Since all smart things are furry (statement 8), and Gary is smart, he\'s furry. But being furry doesn\'t necessarily mean he\'s big. So we can\'t conclude he\'s big.\n\nWait, but statement 12 says cold, white things are furry. So if Gary is cold and white, he\'s furry. But we already know he\'s furry because he\'s smart. So that doesn\'t help us determine if he\'s cold or white.\n\nI think the key is that we don\'t have any information about Gary\'s color or size beyond what\'s given. We know he\'s smart and furry, but nothing about his size. So we can\'t conclude he\'s big.\n\nWait, but statement 9 says big, blue things are round. So if Gary is big and blue, he\'s round. But we don\'t know if he\'s blue. So maybe he\'s not big because we don\'t have evidence he\'s blue.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a way to infer that he\'s blue because of statement 13, which says round things are blue. But we don\'t know if he\'s round.\n\nWait, let\'s think differently. If Gary is big, then he must be blue (from statement 9). But if he\'s blue, then he\'s round (from statement 9). If he\'s round, then he\'s cold (from statement 11). But we don\'t know if he\'s big, so this doesn\'t help.\n\nI think the answer is that we don\'t have enough information to conclude that Gary is big. So the statement is unknown.\n\nWait, but let me check again. The question is whether Gary is big. From the given statements, the only way Gary can be big is if he\'s blue and big, making him round, which makes him cold. But we don\'t know if he\'s blue or cold. So we can\'t conclude he\'s big. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 21%|##        | 125/600 [52:50<41:34,  5.25s/it]
