messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nThe bald eagle is big. The bald eagle likes the cow. The bald eagle sees the rabbit. The cow is big. The cow sees the bald eagle. The dog is big. The dog is young. The dog likes the bald eagle. The rabbit sees the bald eagle. The rabbit visits the bald eagle. If the bald eagle sees the rabbit then the rabbit is big. If something likes the bald eagle then it is blue. If something likes the rabbit and the rabbit visits the cow then the cow is young. If something visits the rabbit then it is round. If something is round and it sees the cow then the cow sees the rabbit. If something is big then it visits the cow. If something sees the rabbit then the rabbit visits the bald eagle. If something is blue then it likes the rabbit. If something likes the cow and the cow is young then it is blue.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The bald eagle likes the rabbit.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown. I think the correct option is: B\n', '<think>.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "The bald eagle likes the rabbit" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context. Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the given statements to make it clearer:.\n\n1. The bald eagle is big.\n2. The bald eagle likes the cow.\n3. The bald eagle sees the rabbit.\n4. The cow is big.\n5. The cow sees the bald eagle.\n6. The dog is big.\n7. The dog is young.\n8. The dog likes the bald eagle.\n9. The rabbit sees the bald eagle.\n10. The rabbit visits the bald eagle.\n11. If the bald eagle sees the rabbit, then the rabbit is big.\n12. If something likes the bald eagle, then it is blue.\n13. If something likes the rabbit and the rabbit visits the cow, then the cow is young.\n14. If something visits the rabbit, then it is round.\n15. If something is round and it sees the cow, then the cow sees the rabbit.\n16. If something is big, then it visits the cow.\n17. If something sees the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the bald eagle.\n18. If something is blue, then it likes the rabbit.\n19. If something likes the cow and the cow is young, then it is blue.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the bald eagle likes the rabbit. So, I need to see if there\'s a direct or indirect link that confirms or denies this.\n\nLooking at the statements, statement 3 says the bald eagle sees the rabbit. Then, statement 17 says if something sees the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the bald eagle. So, from 3 and 17, we can infer that the rabbit visits the bald eagle.\n\nNow, statement 11 says if the bald eagle sees the rabbit, then the rabbit is big. Since the bald eagle does see the rabbit (statement 3), the rabbit must be big (from 11).\n\nNext, statement 13: If something likes the rabbit and the rabbit visits the cow, then the cow is young. We know the rabbit visits the bald eagle, but does it visit the cow? Statement 9 says the rabbit sees the bald eagle, but that\'s not necessarily the same as visiting. Statement 10 says the rabbit visits the bald eagle. So, the rabbit visits the bald eagle, but we don\'t have information about the rabbit visiting the cow. Therefore, we can\'t apply statement 13 here because the condition about the rabbit visiting the cow isn\'t met.\n\nMoving on, statement 14: If something visits the rabbit, then it is round. The bald eagle sees the rabbit (statement 3), but does the bald eagle visit the rabbit? Statement 17 says the rabbit visits the bald eagle, but that doesn\'t necessarily mean the bald eagle visits the rabbit. So, we don\'t know if the bald eagle visits the rabbit, so we can\'t conclude if the bald eagle is round or not.\n\nStatement 15: If something is round and it sees the cow, then the cow sees the rabbit. Again, we don\'t know if the bald eagle is round, so this doesn\'t help.\n\nStatement 16: If something is big, then it visits the cow. We know the rabbit is big (from statement 11), so the rabbit visits the cow (from 15). But does that help with the bald eagle liking the rabbit? Not directly.\n\nStatement 18: If something is blue, then it likes the rabbit. Statement 12 says if something likes the bald eagle, then it is blue. The bald eagle likes the cow (statement 2), so the cow is blue (from 12). Therefore, the cow likes the rabbit (from 18). But does that mean the bald eagle likes the rabbit? Not necessarily, because the cow is a different entity.\n\nStatement 19: If something likes the cow and the cow is young, then it is blue. The cow is big (statement 4), so it\'s not young. Therefore, this statement doesn\'t apply to the cow.\n\nPutting it all together, the only direct information we have is that the bald eagle sees the rabbit, which makes the rabbit big. However, there\'s no direct statement that links the bald eagle liking the rabbit. The only thing we know is that the bald eagle likes the cow, but there\'s no information about the bald eagle liking the rabbit. Therefore, we can\'t conclude whether the bald eagle likes the rabbit or not based on the given information. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 22%|##1       | 129/600 [53:15<34:34,  4.40s/it]
