\nFact 18: If someone visits the bear, they are kind.\n\nWait, maybe I should look for connections between the mouse and the bear. The mouse likes the bear (fact 8), and the bear likes the squirrel (fact 3). The mouse is red (fact 6). The mouse eats the tiger (fact 5). The mouse does not like the tiger (fact 9). The squirrel likes the bear (fact 10). The bear visits the squirrel (fact 4). The bear likes the squirrel (fact 3). So, the bear and squirrel have a liking relationship.\n\nNow, the mouse is red and eats the tiger. The mouse is visited by someone who likes the squirrel (fact 12). So, if the mouse is visited, the person likes the squirrel. But does that affect the tiger\'s preference?.\n\nWait, the mouse eats the tiger, so the tiger is eaten by the mouse. The mouse is red, so maybe the tiger\'s color is red? But fact 2 says the bear is not red. It doesn\'t say anything about the tiger\'s color. So, the tiger could be red or not.\n\nBut the mouse is red and eats the tiger. So, the tiger is eaten by the mouse, which is red. The mouse is not red? Wait, no, fact 6 says the mouse is red. So, the mouse is red and eats the tiger. So, the tiger is eaten by the mouse, which is red.\n\nNow, the mouse is visited by someone who likes the squirrel (fact 12). So, if the mouse is visited, the person likes the squirrel. But does that mean the mouse is being visited by the tiger? Because the mouse eats the tiger, so maybe the tiger visits the mouse? Or is it the other way around?.\n\nWait, the mouse eats the tiger, so the tiger is prey, not visitor. So, the tiger doesn\'t visit the mouse. The mouse is visited by someone else, perhaps.\n\nBut the mouse is red, and if someone is green, they visit the bear (fact 13). So, if someone is green, they visit the bear, which is kind (fact 18). But I don\'t know if the mouse is green or not. The mouse is red, so fact 13 doesn\'t apply to the mouse.\n\nWait, the mouse is red, so if someone is green, they visit the bear. But the mouse is 