ases bear.\n3. Bear chases cow (15).\n4. Tiger sees cow (7).\n5. Tiger chases bear (4).\n6. If tiger is chased by someone (which is the bear), then tiger eats bear (11). So, tiger eats bear.\n7. Therefore, bear is eaten by tiger.\n8. Bald eagle is chasing bear, but bear is dead because tiger ate it. So, the bald eagle is chasing a dead bear, which doesn\'t necessarily mean it\'s eating it.\n\nBut the question is about whether the bald eagle does not eat the bear. So, if the bear is dead, the bald eagle can\'t eat it. But the statement is about whether the bald eagle does not eat the bear, regardless of whether the bear is alive or dead.\n\nWait, but the fact that the bear is being eaten by the tiger doesn\'t directly affect whether the bald eagle eats the bear. The bald eagle is chasing the bear, but the bear is being eaten by the tiger. So, the bald eagle is chasing the bear, but the bear is being eaten by someone else. So, the bald eagle is not eating the bear because the bear is being eaten by the tiger.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bald eagle is chasing the bear, and the bear is being eaten by the tiger, so the bald eagle is not involved in eating the bear. Therefore, the statement "The bald eagle does not eat the bear" would be true.\n\nWait, but I\'m not sure. Let me think again.\n\nIf the bear is being eaten by the tiger, then the bear is dead. So, the bald eagle is chasing a dead bear. But does that mean the bald eagle is eating the bear? No, because the bear is dead. So, the bald eagle is not eating the bear because the bear is dead. Therefore, the statement is true.\n\nAlternatively, if the bear is alive, the bald eagle is chasing it, but the tiger is also chasing the bear, leading to the tiger eating the bear. So, the bear is dead, so the bald eagle is not eating it. Therefore, the statement is true.\n\nWait, but the question is whether the bald eagle does not eat the bear. So, if the bear is dead, the bald eagle can\'t eat it, so the statement is true. If