oint 16 says blue and red things are smart. So, if something is blue or red, it\'s smart. So, Charlie is red, so he\'s smart. Erin is blue, so she\'s smart. Gary is blue, so he\'s smart. Fiona is big and green. We don\'t know if she\'s blue or red yet.\n\nWait, but from point 13, if something is red and green, then it\'s blue. So, if something is red, it\'s green, and if it\'s red and green, it\'s blue. So, red implies blue. So, red and blue are the same. Therefore, red, blue, and green are all the same category.\n\nWait, that can\'t be because Charlie is red, blue, and green, but Erin and Gary are blue and green. So, maybe red, blue, and green are all the same. So, any red thing is blue and green, and any blue thing is red and green, and any green thing is red and blue.\n\nWait, that might make sense. So, red, blue, and green are all the same category. So, any red thing is blue and green, and any blue thing is red and green, and any green thing is red and blue.\n\nIf that\'s the case, then all red, blue, and green things are the same. So, Charlie is red, so he\'s blue and green. Erin is blue, so she\'s red and green. Gary is blue, so he\'s red and green. Fiona is big and green, so she\'s red and blue.\n\nWait, but point 13 says if something is red and green, then it\'s blue. But if red and green are the same as red, then being red and green is just red, so if something is red, it\'s blue. So, red implies blue.\n\nSimilarly, point 15 says if something is blue and green, then it\'s smart. But if blue and green are the same as blue, then if something is blue, it\'s smart.\n\nWait, but point 16 says blue and red things are smart. So, if something is blue or red, it\'s smart. But if red and blue are the same, then if something is red or blue, it\'s smart. So, all red, blue, and green things are smart.\n\nBut let\'s get back to the question: Is Fiona not blue?.\n\nFiona is big and green. From the above, if green is the same as red and blue, then Fiona is red, blue, and green. So, Fiona is blue. Therefore, the statement "Fiona is not blue" would be false.\n\nWait, but let me double-check.\n\nIf red, blue, and green are all the same category, then Fiona being green would mean she\'s red and blue. So, she\'s blue. Therefore, the statement "Fiona is not blue" would be false.\n\nAlternatively, if red, blue, and green are separate, but red implies green, and if something is red and green, it\'s blue. So, if Fiona is green, she could be red and green, making her blue. So, she\'s blue. Therefore, the statement "Fiona is not blue" is false.\n\nWait, but let me think again. If red implies green, and if something is red and green, it\'s blue. So, if Fiona is green, she could be red and green, making her blue. Therefore, Fiona is blue. So, the statement "Fiona is not blue" is false.\n\nAlternatively, if red, blue, and green are separate, but red implies green, and if something is red and green, it\'s blue. So, if Fiona is green, she could be red and green, making her blue. So, she\'s blue. Therefore, the statement is false.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any information that suggests Fiona is not blue. From the given, Fiona is big and green. There\'s no information that says she\'s not blue. So, based on the rules, if she\'s green and red (because red implies green), then she\'s blue. Therefore, she must be blue. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 22%|##2       | 134/600 [53:30<24:06,  3.10s/it]
