e bald eagle.\n16. If something visits the cat, then it visits the rabbit.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the rabbit is big. So, I need to see if the given information leads us to conclude that the rabbit is big, or if it\'s false, or if it\'s unknown.\n\nLet\'s break it down.\n\nFirst, from statement 4: The cat visits the rabbit. From statement 16: If something visits the cat, then it visits the rabbit. So, the cat visiting the rabbit is consistent with this rule, but it doesn\'t directly tell us about the rabbit\'s size.\n\nLooking at statement 12: If something is nice, then it is big. So, to say the rabbit is big, we need to know if the rabbit is nice.\n\nFrom statement 13: If something visits the bald eagle, then it is nice. So, if the rabbit visits the bald eagle, then the rabbit is nice.\n\nFrom statement 7: The rabbit eats the bald eagle. So, the rabbit does visit the bald eagle (since eating implies visiting). Therefore, according to statement 13, the rabbit is nice.\n\nSince the rabbit is nice (from statement 13), from statement 12, the rabbit must be big.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Statement 13 says if something visits the bald eagle, then it is nice. The rabbit eats the bald eagle, which means it visits the bald eagle. So yes, the rabbit is nice. Then, statement 12 says if something is nice, it\'s big. So the rabbit is big. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
