messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nThe dog does not eat the rabbit. The mouse eats the tiger. The mouse is green. The rabbit does not chase the tiger. The rabbit eats the dog. The rabbit is young. The tiger is green. If something visits the mouse then the mouse is big. If something eats the tiger then the tiger visits the mouse. If the dog is young and the dog does not visit the mouse then the mouse does not visit the rabbit. If something is green and it chases the rabbit then the rabbit does not eat the mouse. If something is green then it visits the dog. If something visits the rabbit and the rabbit is young then it is round. If something is round and it visits the dog then it chases the tiger. If something is big then it visits the rabbit.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The tiger is not green.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown. I think the correct option is: A\n', '<think>.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "The tiger is not green" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context. Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the given statements to make it easier:.\n\n1. The dog does not eat the rabbit.\n2. The mouse eats the tiger.\n3. The mouse is green.\n4. The rabbit does not chase the tiger.\n5. The rabbit eats the dog.\n6. The rabbit is young.\n7. The tiger is green.\n8. If something visits the mouse, then the mouse is big.\n9. If something eats the tiger, then the tiger visits the mouse.\n10. If the dog is young and the dog does not visit the mouse, then the mouse does not visit the rabbit.\n11. If something is green and it chases the rabbit, then the rabbit does not eat the mouse.\n12. If something is green, then it visits the dog.\n13. If something visits the rabbit and the rabbit is young, then it is round.\n14. If something is round and it visits the dog, then it chases the tiger.\n15. If something is big, then it visits the rabbit.\n\nWait, hold on. Point 7 says "The tiger is green." So according to that, the tiger is green. But the question is asking if the tiger is not green. So if the tiger is green, then the statement "The tiger is not green" would be false.\n\nBut let me make sure I\'m not missing anything. Maybe there\'s a contradiction or something else that could change this conclusion.\n\nLooking at point 3: "The mouse is green." So the mouse is green. Point 9 says if something eats the tiger, then the tiger visits the mouse. But point 2 says the mouse eats the tiger, so that would mean the tiger visits the mouse. But point 7 already states the tiger is green, so that\'s consistent.\n\nPoint 12 says if something is green, it visits the dog. Since the tiger is green (point 7), the tiger would visit the dog. But point 15 says if something is big, it visits the rabbit. I don\'t know if the tiger is big yet.\n\nPoint 14: If something is round and visits the dog, it chases the tiger. But I don\'t know if the tiger is round.\n\nPoint 10: If the dog is young and doesn\'t visit the mouse, then the mouse doesn\'t visit the rabbit. But I don\'t know the dog\'s age or if it visits the mouse.\n\nPoint 6: The rabbit is young. Point 13 says if something visits the rabbit and the rabbit is young, then it\'s round. So the rabbit is round because it\'s young and is visited by something. But who visits the rabbit? Maybe the mouse or the dog.\n\nPoint 1: The dog doesn\'t eat the rabbit. Point 5: The rabbit eats the dog. So the rabbit eats the dog, which means the dog is alive, but I don\'t know if it\'s young or old.\n\nPoint 4: The rabbit does not chase the tiger. So the rabbit is not chasing the tiger, but the mouse is green (point 3) and the mouse eats the tiger (point 2). So the mouse is eating the tiger, which might mean the mouse is big (point 8), but I don\'t know if the mouse is big.\n\nWait, point 8 says if something visits the mouse, then the mouse is big. So if the mouse is big, it must have visited something. But the mouse is eating the tiger, so maybe the mouse visited the tiger? Or maybe the mouse is big because it visited something else.\n\nBut the key point is that the tiger is green according to point 7, so the statement "The tiger is not green" would be false. Unless there\'s a contradiction somewhere.\n\nLet me check if any other points contradict this. Point 11 says if something is green and chases the rabbit, then the rabbit doesn\'t eat the mouse. But the rabbit is young (point 6), so maybe it\'s round (point 13). If the rabbit is round and visits the dog (point 12), then it chases the tiger (point 14). But the tiger is green, so if the rabbit chases the tiger, does that affect anything? I\'m not sure.\n\nBut the main point is that the tiger is explicitly stated as green in point 7. So unless there\'s a rule that changes this, the tiger being green makes the statement "The tiger is not green" false.\n\nI think I\'m overcomplicating it. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 23%|##3       | 139/600 [53:35<09:38,  1.25s/it]
