\'t directly affect Fiona\'s color. However, from point 12, being quiet implies she\'s green. But let\'s check if there\'s any contradiction.\n\nWait, point 16 says that rough, nice things are quiet. But Fiona is rough and not nice, so that rule doesn\'t apply to her. So, being rough and not nice doesn\'t make her quiet or affect her color directly.\n\nBut from point 12, quiet things are green. So Fiona, being quiet, should be green. But the question is asking if Fiona is not green. So if she\'s quiet, she must be green, making the statement "Fiona is not green" false.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Are there any other rules that might affect her color? For example, point 10 says if something is round and blue, then it\'s rough. But Fiona is round and rough, but she\'s not blue because she\'s not nice, and all nice things are blue (point 14). So Fiona isn\'t nice, so she\'s not blue. Therefore, she\'s round, rough, and not blue. But from point 13, round things are green. So being round would make her green, regardless of being blue or not.\n\nSo, yes, Fiona is green because she\'s quiet, and quiet things are green. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
