bit doesn\'t visit the squirrel. But does that mean the rabbit visits the mouse? Not necessarily. The rabbit could visit the mouse, but statement 9 only says it doesn\'t visit the squirrel.\n\nWait, statement 7 says the rabbit is rough. Statement 16 says if something is rough, it visits the mouse. So the rabbit, being rough, must visit the mouse. So the rabbit visits the mouse.\n\nNow, using statement 17: If something (the rabbit) needs the mouse and it (the rabbit) visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the rabbit. So, since the rabbit needs the mouse (from statement 12, the squirrel needs the mouse, but does that mean the mouse needs the squirrel? Or is it the other way around? Wait, statement 12 says the squirrel needs the mouse, which might mean the mouse is needed by the squirrel, but not necessarily that the mouse needs the squirrel. Hmm, that\'s a bit confusing.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m misinterpreting. Let me clarify: "The squirrel needs the mouse" could mean that the mouse is needed by the squirrel, i.e., the squirrel requires the mouse. So, the mouse is needed by the squirrel, but does that mean the mouse needs the squirrel? Not necessarily. It\'s a bit ambiguous, but in logical terms, "A needs B" could mean B is required by A, so A requires B, but not necessarily that B requires A.\n\nBut according to statement 17, if the rabbit needs the mouse and the rabbit visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the rabbit. So, the rabbit needs the mouse (from statement 12, since the squirrel needs the mouse, but does that imply the rabbit needs the mouse? Or is it the other way around? Wait, statement 12 says the squirrel needs the mouse, but the rabbit\'s need for the mouse isn\'t directly stated.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m overcomplicating. Let\'s break it down:.\n\n1. The rabbit is rough (statement 7).\n2. If something is rough, it visits the mouse (statement 16). So the rabbit visits the mouse.\n3. Now, statement 17: If something (the rabbit) needs the mouse and it (the rabbit