Charlie is red, he\'s also furry.\n\nFrom point 9, all furry things are red, which we already have from point 8, so that\'s consistent.\n\nFrom point 12, all furry things are green. Since Charlie is furry, he must be green.\n\nWait, but the statement is "Charlie is not green." If Charlie is green, then the statement is false.\n\nBut let me check if there\'s any contradiction or if I missed something.\n\nPoint 13 says if something is red and green, then it\'s blue. But Charlie is red and, from point 12, he\'s also green. So according to point 13, Charlie should be blue. But that\'s a contradiction because Charlie is red and also blue? That can\'t be right.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake. Let me re-examine.\n\nPoint 12: All furry things are green. So since Charlie is furry, he\'s green.\n\nPoint 13: If something is red and green, then it\'s blue. So if Charlie is red and green, he must be blue. But that would mean Charlie is both red and blue, which might not be possible unless blue and red are the same, but that\'s not stated.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m misapplying the logic. Let\'s see:.\n\nCharlie is young → red (point 10).\n\nRed things are furry (point 8).\n\nFurry things are green (point 12). So Charlie is green.\n\nBut point 13 says if something is red and green, then it\'s blue. So if Charlie is red and green, he must be blue. But that would mean Charlie is blue, which contradicts him being red.\n\nHmm, this seems like a contradiction. Maybe I\'m misunderstanding point 13.\n\nWait, point 13: If something is red and green, then it is blue. So if a thing is red and green, it must be blue. But Charlie is red and green, so he must be blue. But from point 10, he\'s red, and from point 12, he\'s green. So he\'s both red and green, which according to point 13, he must be blue. But that would mean he\'s blue, which contradicts him being red.\n\nThis seems like a problem. Maybe the context has an inconsistency. Alternatively, perhaps I\'m misapplying the logic.\n\nWait, maybe the points are meant to be applied in a certain order or with certain conditions. Let me try to map it out.\n\nCharlie is young → red (point 10).\n\nRed things are furry (point 8) → Charlie is furry.\n\nFurry things are green (point 12) → Charlie is green.\n\nSo Charlie is green.\n\nBut point 13: If something is red and green, then it is blue. So if Charlie is red and green, he must be blue. But he\'s already green, so being red and green would require him to be blue. But he\'s red, so that\'s a contradiction.\n\nWait, maybe the issue is that Charlie can\'t be both red and blue. So perhaps the conclusion is that Charlie is red and green, which makes him blue, but that\'s a contradiction, so maybe Charlie isn\'t red? But that contradicts point 10, which says all young things are red.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the statements are such that Charlie can\'t exist under these rules, making the scenario impossible. But that\'s probably not the case.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m overcomplicating it. Let\'s try to see if Charlie can be both red and green, which would make him blue, but that\'s a contradiction. So perhaps the only way to resolve this is to say that Charlie is green, making the statement "Charlie is not green" false.\n\nBut then point 13 would require him to be blue, which contradicts him being red. So maybe the only way to resolve this is to say that Charlie is green, making the statement false, but then point 13 is problematic.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the statements are such that if something is red and green, it\'s blue, but Charlie is red and green, so he\'s blue. But that would mean he\'s blue, which contradicts him being red. So perhaps the only way to resolve this is to say that Charlie is blue, but that contradicts him being red.\n\nWait, maybe the key is that Charlie is both red and green, which makes him blue, but that\'s impossible, so perhaps the only way is that Charlie is green, making the statement "Charlie is not green" false.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the