asing the lion, but the lion isn\'t chasing the bear. So, does that mean the lion doesn\'t visit the bear? Or does visiting mean something else?.\n\nWait, visiting could mean going near or interacting, but the facts don\'t specify that. The facts only talk about chasing and seeing. So, the bear is chasing the lion, but the lion isn\'t chasing the bear. So, maybe the lion doesn\'t visit the bear because the bear is chasing it, and the lion isn\'t chasing back. Alternatively, visiting could mean seeing, but the facts don\'t say that.\n\nWait, let\'s think about the rules again. The squirrel sees the dog (fact 16), so the squirrel visits the bear (fact 21). So, the squirrel sees the bear. Now, from fact 17, if something visits the bear, it sees the bear. So, the squirrel sees the bear. Now, from fact 18, if the bear sees the dog, then the dog sees the bear. So, the dog sees the bear. Now, from fact 19, if the dog is young and visits the dog, then the dog sees the bear. But we don\'t know if the dog is young. So, maybe the dog is not young, so fact 19 doesn\'t apply. Therefore, the dog sees the bear because the bear sees the dog, but fact 19 doesn\'t apply.\n\nNow, the lion is being chased by the bear, but the lion isn\'t chasing the bear. So, does that mean the lion doesn\'t visit the bear? Or is there a way the lion could visit the bear? The facts don\'t say anything about the lion visiting the bear directly. The only interaction is the bear chasing the lion, but the lion isn\'t chasing the bear. So, maybe the lion doesn\'t visit the bear.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the lion visits the bear because the dog sees the lion (fact 7), and the dog sees the bear (from fact 18, since the bear sees the dog). So, the dog sees the bear, which means the dog visits the bear (from fact 21). Wait, no, fact 21 says if something sees the dog, it visits the bear. So, the dog sees the bear (from fact 18), so the dog visits the bear (from fact 21). So, the dog visits the bear. Now, the dog 