messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nAnne is big. Anne is kind. Charlie is big. Erin is big. Erin is blue. Erin is not kind. Erin is not young. Harry is big. Harry is nice. Harry is white. Harry is young. All kind, big things are blue. If something is young and kind then it is nice. Blue, cold things are nice. White things are cold. White, big things are cold. If Erin is blue and Erin is white then Erin is nice. All cold, nice things are young. All kind, blue things are white.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Harry is white.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown. I think the correct option is: C\n', '<think>.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "Harry is white" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context. Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the facts provided:.\n\n1. Anne is big.\n2. Anne is kind.\n3. Charlie is big.\n4. Erin is big.\n5. Erin is blue.\n6. Erin is not kind.\n7. Erin is not young.\n8. Harry is big.\n9. Harry is nice.\n10. Harry is white.\n11. All kind, big things are blue.\n12. If something is young and kind, then it is nice.\n13. Blue, cold things are nice.\n14. White things are cold.\n15. White, big things are cold.\n16. If Erin is blue and Erin is white, then Erin is nice.\n17. All cold, nice things are young.\n18. All kind, blue things are white.\n\nWait, hold on. There\'s a contradiction here. Let me check the details again.\n\nLooking at point 10, it says Harry is white. But point 14 says white things are cold. Then point 15 says white, big things are cold. So Harry is white, so he\'s cold. Now, point 17 says all cold, nice things are young. But point 9 says Harry is nice. So if Harry is nice and cold, then according to point 17, he should be young. But point 14 says white things are cold, and point 10 says Harry is white, so he\'s cold. But point 12 says blue, cold things are nice. So if Harry is white, he\'s cold, but point 12 is about blue, cold things being nice. So does that affect Harry?.\n\nWait, let me think again. The question is about Harry\'s color. The given information says Harry is white (point 10). So according to the facts, Harry is white. But let me check if there\'s any other information that might affect this.\n\nPoint 14: White things are cold. So Harry is white, so he\'s cold. Point 15: White, big things are cold. Harry is white and big, so he\'s cold. Point 17: All cold, nice things are young. Harry is nice and cold, so he must be young. But point 18 says all kind, blue things are white. So if something is kind and blue, it\'s white. But Erin is blue and not kind (point 6), so that\'s fine. Harry is white, so if he\'s kind, he should be blue, but point 9 says Harry is nice, not necessarily kind. Wait, point 11 says all kind, big things are blue. So if Harry is kind and big, he should be blue. But Harry is white, so he can\'t be kind and blue at the same time. So maybe Harry isn\'t kind.\n\nBut the question is only about Harry\'s color. The given information explicitly says Harry is white (point 10). So regardless of other implications, the statement "Harry is white" is directly stated as true. Unless there\'s a contradiction elsewhere that affects this, but I don\'t see any. The other points are about other entities like Anne, Charlie, Erin, and their properties, but they don\'t contradict Harry\'s color.\n\nWait, but let me check point 16: If Erin is blue and Erin is white, then Erin is nice. But Erin is blue (point 5) and white? Wait, point 14 says white things are cold, and point 15 says white, big things are cold. But Erin is big (point 4) and blue (point 5). So Erin is big and blue, which makes her cold (from point 15). But point 14 says white things are cold, so if Erin were white, she\'d be cold. But she\'s blue, so she\'s cold. But point 16 says if Erin is blue and white, then she\'s nice. But Erin is only blue, not white, so that condition isn\'t met. So Erin isn\'t white, so point 16 doesn\'t apply to her.\n\nSo, going back, Harry is explicitly stated as white (point 10). Therefore, my final option is: ']] 30%|###       | 182/600 [1:05:16<28:30,  4.09s/it] 30%|###       | 183/600 [1:05:17<20:48,  2.99s/it]
