messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nThe bald eagle eats the cow. The bald eagle does not eat the dog. The bald eagle is rough. The cow is round. The cow sees the bald eagle. The cow does not see the dog. The cow visits the bald eagle. The cow visits the lion. The dog is rough. The lion is young. The lion does not see the bald eagle. The lion sees the cow. If someone is green and they eat the bald eagle then the bald eagle is not rough. If someone is big and they do not see the bald eagle then the bald eagle is rough. If someone is big then they visit the dog. If someone eats the lion and they are big then the lion eats the dog. If someone visits the dog then the dog eats the cow. If someone is rough and they eat the cow then they are young. If the lion eats the cow then the lion visits the bald eagle. If someone is big and they see the lion then they are green. If someone is young then they are big.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The lion is big.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown. I think the correct option is: B\n', "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out whether the lion is big based on the given context. Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I'll list out all the given statements to make it easier to reference:\n\n1. The bald eagle eats the cow.\n2. The bald eagle does not eat the dog.\n3. The bald eagle is rough.\n4. The cow is round.\n5. The cow sees the bald eagle.\n6. The cow does not see the dog.\n7. The cow visits the bald eagle.\n8. The cow visits the lion.\n9. The dog is rough.\n10. The lion is young.\n11. The lion does not see the bald eagle.\n12. The lion sees the cow.\n13. If someone is green and they eat the bald eagle, then the bald eagle is not rough.\n14. If someone is big and they do not see the bald eagle, then the bald eagle is rough.\n15. If someone is big, then they visit the dog.\n16. If someone eats the lion and they are big, then the lion eats the dog.\n17. If someone visits the dog, then the dog eats the cow.\n18. If someone is rough and they eat the cow, then they are young.\n19. If the lion eats the cow, then the lion visits the bald eagle.\n20. If someone is big and they see the lion, then they are green.\n21. If someone is young, then they are big.\n\nNow, the question is: Is the lion big?\n\nLet's break this down. From statement 10, the lion is young. From statement 21, if someone is young, then they are big. So, the lion being young implies that the lion is big. Therefore, the lion is big.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any contradiction or if I'm missing something. Statement 10 says the lion is young, and statement 21 says that being young implies being big. So, yes, the lion must be big.\n\nI don't see any statements that contradict this. So, the lion is indeed big.\n. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
