is also young.\n\nNow, does the tiger see the dog? Let\'s look at statement 11: If something sees the bald eagle, it eats the cow. So, if the tiger sees the bald eagle, it would eat the cow. But the tiger already eats the cow, so maybe the tiger sees the bald eagle. But does that mean it sees the dog?.\n\nAlternatively, from statement 13: If something sees the tiger, it needs the bald eagle. So, if the dog sees the tiger, it needs the bald eagle. But we don\'t know if the dog sees the tiger.\n\nWait, but the dog eats the tiger, so maybe the dog sees the tiger? Or maybe not necessarily. The fact that the dog eats the tiger doesn\'t imply that the dog sees it. So, perhaps the dog doesn\'t see the tiger, but we don\'t have information about that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger sees the dog. But we don\'t have any direct information about that.\n\nWait, let\'s think about the relationships again. The tiger eats the cow, the dog eats the bald eagle, the tiger needs the bald eagle, the dog is red, the tiger is red, the dog eats the tiger, making the dog young.\n\nBut the question is about the tiger seeing the dog. So, do we have any information that links the tiger seeing the dog?.\n\nFrom statement 11: If something sees the bald eagle, it eats the cow. So, if the tiger sees the bald eagle, it would eat the cow, which it already does. But that doesn\'t necessarily mean it sees the dog.\n\nAlternatively, from statement 13: If something sees the tiger, it needs the bald eagle. So, if the dog sees the tiger, it needs the bald eagle. But we don\'t know if the dog sees the tiger.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger sees the dog. But there\'s no direct statement about that.\n\nWait, perhaps the tiger sees the dog because the dog is red, but I don\'t think that\'s a given.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger doesn\'t see the dog because the dog is red and the tiger is young, but I don\'t think that\'s relevant.\n\nWait, maybe the key is that the dog is red, so from statement 10, 