ess specified.\n\nLooking at statement 17: If something needs the squirrel, the squirrel visits the mouse. But I don\'t see anything about something needing the squirrel. Statement 16: If something visits the bear and the bear visits the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the rabbit. But I don\'t know if the bear visits the squirrel. Statement 15: If something chases the bear, then it is cold. The mouse chases the bear (2), so the mouse is cold. Statement 13: If something visits the mouse and the mouse needs the squirrel, then it needs the rabbit. But does the mouse need the squirrel? I don\'t think so. The mouse is being chased by the bear and chases the rabbit. .\n\nWait, maybe the bear is cold because the mouse chases it (statement 15). So the bear is cold. Statement 13: If something visits the mouse and the mouse needs the squirrel, then it needs the rabbit. But the mouse is being chased by the bear, so maybe the mouse needs the bear? Or is the mouse being chased by the bear, making the bear cold, but does that make the mouse need the squirrel? I\'m getting confused.\n\nLet me try to connect the dots. The mouse chases the bear (2), so the mouse is cold (from 15). The mouse chases the rabbit (3), and the mouse visits the rabbit (4). The rabbit needs the mouse (6). The squirrel chases the mouse (7) and the rabbit (8). The squirrel is young (11). .\n\nIf the squirrel is young and needs the rabbit, it visits the rabbit (12). But does the squirrel need the rabbit? I don\'t see that. Statement 17: If something needs the squirrel, the squirrel visits the mouse. But I don\'t know if anything needs the squirrel. .\n\nWait, maybe the bear is cold (from 15), so it needs the squirrel (from 13: if something is cold, it needs the squirrel). So the bear needs the squirrel. Then, from statement 17: if the bear needs the squirrel, the squirrel visits the mouse. But does that help? .\n\nIf the squirrel visits the mouse, and the mouse needs the squirrel, then from statement 14: if something visits the mouse and the mouse needs the squirrel, then it needs the rabbit. So, if the squirrel visits the mouse, and the mouse needs the squirrel, then the squirrel needs the rabbit. But does the mouse need the squirrel? I don\'t think so. The mouse is being chased by the bear and chases the rabbit. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bear is cold, so it needs the squirrel (from 13). Then, the squirrel needs the mouse (from 17). Then, the mouse needs the rabbit (from 6). So, the bear needs the squirrel, which needs the mouse, which needs the rabbit. So, indirectly, the bear needs the rabbit. .\n\nWait, but does the squirrel need the mouse? Statement 17 says if something needs the squirrel, the squirrel visits the mouse. But does the squirrel need the mouse? Or is it the other way around? The squirrel chases the mouse, but does that mean the squirrel needs the mouse? Or is it just chasing it? .\n\nI think chasing doesn\'t necessarily mean needing. So maybe the squirrel just chases the mouse, but doesn\'t need it. So, the squirrel visiting the mouse might not imply that the mouse needs the squirrel. .\n\nAlternatively, if the bear is cold, it needs the squirrel (13). Then, the squirrel needs the mouse (17). Then, the mouse needs the rabbit (6). So, the bear needs the squirrel, which needs the mouse, which needs the rabbit. Therefore, the bear indirectly needs the rabbit. .\n\nBut I\'m not entirely sure because the chaining of needs might not be direct. So, the bear needs the squirrel, which needs the mouse, which needs the rabbit. So, the bear needs the rabbit through the squirrel and mouse. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bear doesn\'t need the rabbit directly, but through the squirrel and mouse. So, the statement "The bear needs the rabbit" would be true because of the chain of dependencies. .\n\nWait, but let me check again. The bear is cold because the mouse chases it (15). The bear being cold needs the squirrel (13). The squirrel needs the mouse (17). The mouse needs the rabbit (6). So, the chain is bear -> squirrel -> mouse -> rabbit. Therefore, the bear indirectly needs the rabbit. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 46%|####6     | 276/600 [1:23:00<10:59,  2.04s/it]
