messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nBob is big. Charlie is furry. Charlie is kind. Charlie is smart. Fiona is furry. Fiona is young. Gary is smart. Big, smart things are kind. If something is red and not smart then it is kind. Smart things are furry. If something is red and big then it is furry. If Charlie is red then Charlie is kind. If something is kind then it is young. Young things are not red. If something is furry and smart then it is big.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? Fiona is not smart.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown. I think the correct option is: B\n', '<think>.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "Fiona is not smart" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context. Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the facts provided:.\n\n1. Bob is big.\n2. Charlie is furry.\n3. Charlie is kind.\n4. Charlie is smart.\n5. Fiona is furry.\n6. Fiona is young.\n7. Gary is smart.\n8. Big, smart things are kind.\n9. If something is red and not smart, then it is kind.\n10. Smart things are furry.\n11. If something is red and big, then it is furry.\n12. If Charlie is red, then Charlie is kind.\n13. If something is kind, then it is young.\n14. Young things are not red.\n15. If something is furry and smart, then it is big.\n\nThe question is about Fiona\'s smartness. So, I need to see if we can determine whether Fiona is smart or not based on these statements.\n\nLooking at the facts, Fiona is listed as being furry (fact 5) and young (fact 6). Now, let\'s see if there\'s any information about her being smart or not.\n\nFrom fact 10: Smart things are furry. So, if something is smart, it must be furry. But this doesn\'t necessarily mean that all furry things are smart. So, being furry doesn\'t automatically make someone smart.\n\nFact 15 says that if something is furry and smart, then it is big. So, if Fiona were smart, she would have to be big. But from the facts, Fiona is young (fact 6). Fact 14 says that young things are not red. But that doesn\'t directly relate to her being big or not.\n\nWait, let\'s think about the implications. If something is smart, it\'s furry (fact 10). If something is furry and smart, it\'s big (fact 15). So, if Fiona were smart, she\'d have to be big. But Fiona is young (fact 6), and from fact 14, young things are not red. But I don\'t see a direct link between age and size here.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s look at the other direction. If something is big, it\'s smart because big, smart things are kind (fact 8). But Bob is big (fact 1), so Bob is smart. But that\'s about Bob, not Fiona.\n\nWait, let\'s see if there\'s any information about Fiona\'s smartness. The only direct fact is that Fiona is furry and young. There\'s no explicit statement about her being smart or not. So, can we infer anything?.\n\nFact 10 says smart things are furry, but that doesn\'t mean all furry things are smart. So, Fiona being furry doesn\'t necessarily mean she\'s smart. Similarly, fact 15 says that if she were smart, she\'d have to be big, but she\'s young, so maybe she\'s not big, but that doesn\'t necessarily mean she\'s not smart.\n\nWait, but let\'s think about the contrapositive. If something is not smart, then it\'s kind only if it\'s red (from fact 9). But we don\'t know if Fiona is red. The only thing we know is that if Charlie is red, he\'s kind (fact 12). But we don\'t know if Charlie is red or not. So, that might not help.\n\nAlternatively, since Fiona is young (fact 6), and from fact 13, if something is kind, it\'s young. So, being young doesn\'t necessarily make something kind, but being kind does make something young. So, Fiona being young doesn\'t tell us if she\'s kind or not.\n\nWait, but fact 13 says if something is kind, then it\'s young. So, being young is a necessary condition for being kind. But it\'s not sufficient. So, being young doesn\'t mean you\'re kind.\n\nSo, putting it all together, we know Fiona is young and furry. We don\'t have any direct information about her being smart or not. The only connection is that if she were smart, she\'d have to be big (fact 15), but she\'s young, which might suggest she\'s not big, but that\'s speculative.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can infer that since she\'s young, she\'s not red (fact 14), but that doesn\'t affect her smartness.\n\nSo, I think the key point is that there\'s no direct information about Fiona\'s smartness. The facts only tell us that if she were smart, she\'d have to be big, but she\'s young, which doesn\'t necessarily mean she\'s not smart. Therefore, we can\'t determine for sure whether she\'s smart or not based on the given information.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Fact 10 says smart things are furry, but not all furry things are smart. So, Fiona being furry doesn\'t confirm she\'s smart. Similarly, fact 15 says that if she\'s furry and smart, she\'s big, but she\'s young, so maybe she\'s not big, but that doesn\'t mean she\'s not smart.\n\nWait, but if she were smart, she\'d have to be big, but she\'s young, so maybe she\'s not big, but that doesn\'t necessarily mean she\'s not smart. It\'s possible she\'s smart and not big, but that\'s not covered by the facts.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use the fact that if something is big, it\'s smart (from fact 8). So, if Fiona were big, she\'d be smart. But she\'s young, so maybe she\'s not big. But again, that\'s speculative.\n\nWait, but the facts don\'t say anything about Fiona\'s size. So, we can\'t determine her size based on the given information. Therefore, we can\'t conclude whether she\'s smart or not. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 51%|#####1    | 306/600 [1:23:47<10:11,  2.08s/it]
