te, kind, and smart.\n\nPoint 10 says white things are kind. Well, Bob is white and kind, which fits.\n\nPoint 9 says if Charlie is white, then Charlie is green. But we don\'t know yet if Charlie is white. Let\'s see if Charlie can be white.\n\nFrom point 2, Charlie is furry, and from point 12, all furry things are cold. So Charlie is cold. Now, point 14 says if something is cold and kind, it\'s white. So if Charlie is both cold and kind, then Charlie is white.\n\nWait, Charlie is kind (point 3) and cold (from being furry and all furry things are cold). So according to point 14, Charlie is white.\n\nIf Charlie is white, then from point 9, Charlie is green. So Charlie is both white and green? That seems contradictory because if Charlie is white, he must be green. But the question is whether Charlie is not green. So if Charlie is green, then the statement "Charlie is not green" would be false.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Charlie is furry (point 2), so he\'s a furry thing. All furry things are cold (point 12). So Charlie is cold. Also, Charlie is kind (point 3). From point 14, if something is cold and kind, it\'s white. So Charlie is white.\n\nThen, from point 9, if Charlie is white, he must be green. So Charlie is green. Therefore, the statement "Charlie is not green" would be false because Charlie is green.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I didn\'t miss anything. Let me go through each point again.\n\nCharlie is furry (2). All furry things are cold (12). So Charlie is cold. Charlie is kind (3). So Charlie is cold and kind. From point 14, cold and kind things are white. So Charlie is white. Then, from point 9, if Charlie is white, he is green. So Charlie is green. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
