on sees the dog, but the cat is separate. So, the cat doesn\'t see the lion, so statement 15: If something needs the bald eagle, it sees the lion. But the cat doesn\'t see the lion, so it doesn\'t need the bald eagle. Alternatively, does the cat need the bald eagle because the cat sees the lion? No, because the cat doesn\'t see the lion. The cat is eaten by the dog, which is eaten by the lion, but the cat itself doesn\'t see the lion.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The cat is big (statement 3) and sees the cat (statement 17: if something is big, it sees the cat). Wait, no, statement 17 says if something is big, it sees the cat. So, the cat is big, so it sees the cat? That seems a bit odd, but maybe it\'s a reflexive statement. So, the cat sees itself? Not sure, but perhaps it\'s a way to say that the cat is aware of its own existence, but not necessarily relevant.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the cat sees the lion through the dog? But that\'s not directly stated. The cat is eaten by the dog, which is eaten by the lion, but the cat doesn\'t see the lion. So, the cat doesn\'t see the lion, so it doesn\'t need the dog (from statement 14). Since the cat doesn\'t need the dog, statement 15 says if something needs the bald eagle, it sees the lion. But the cat doesn\'t see the lion, so it doesn\'t need the bald eagle. Therefore, the cat does not need the bald eagle.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any other path. The cat eats the dog (statement 2), and the dog eats the bald eagle (statement 5). So, the cat indirectly eats the bald eagle. But does that mean the cat needs the bald eagle? Or is it just that the cat\'s diet includes the dog, which includes the bald eagle, but the cat itself doesn\'t need the bald eagle because it\'s not part of its diet unless it\'s necessary for survival.\n\nWait, the context says the cat eats the dog, and the dog eats the bald eagle. So, the cat\'s diet includes the dog, which includes the bald eagle. But does that mean the cat needs the 