t needs the cat because it\'s being chased by the cat. But the rabbit is chasing the cat, so it\'s the rabbit moving towards the cat, not the cat moving towards the rabbit.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the rabbit is being chased by the cat, so the rabbit needs the cat because it\'s being chased. But the rabbit is the one moving towards the cat, so maybe it\'s the cat chasing the rabbit, but statement 10 says the rabbit is chasing the cat.\n\nI think I\'m going in circles. Let me try to summarize:.\n\n- The mouse eats the rabbit (statement 8), and the mouse does not need the cat (statement 9). So, when the mouse eats the rabbit, the rabbit doesn\'t need the cat.\n\n- The rabbit chases the cat (statement 10), and the rabbit chases the mouse (statement 11). The rabbit is not big (statement 12).\n\n- From statement 13: If something chases the rabbit, then it needs the cat. But the rabbit is chasing the cat, not something else chasing the rabbit. So, statement 13 doesn\'t apply.\n\n- From statement 16: If something chases the rabbit, then the rabbit is blue. But the rabbit is not big, so maybe it\'s not blue.\n\n- From statement 17: If something needs the cat and it\'s not big, then it\'s kind. So, if the rabbit needs the cat and it\'s not big, then it\'s kind.\n\n- From statement 18: If something is kind and round, then it does not eat the mouse. But we don\'t know if the rabbit is round.\n\nWait, maybe the rabbit is round because the cat is round (statement 3), but that\'s not specified. So, we don\'t know if the rabbit is round.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rabbit is not round, so statement 18 doesn\'t apply.\n\nI think the key point is that the rabbit is being eaten by the mouse, and the mouse does not need the cat. So, when the mouse eats the rabbit, the rabbit doesn\'t need the cat. Therefore, the statement "The rabbit does not need the cat" is true.\n\nWait, but the rabbit is also chasing the cat. So, does that mean the rabbit needs the cat because it\'s being chased 