messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nThe cat chases the dog. The cat is rough. The cat is young. The dog likes the squirrel. The dog sees the tiger. The squirrel chases the tiger. The squirrel is big. The squirrel is green. The squirrel likes the dog. The squirrel sees the dog. The tiger chases the cat. The tiger is green. The tiger likes the dog. The tiger likes the squirrel. If something likes the dog then it chases the tiger. If something is big and it chases the cat then the cat likes the squirrel. If something sees the cat then the cat is big. If something likes the squirrel then it sees the dog. If something is cold and it chases the cat then it sees the cat. If something sees the tiger then the tiger is big. Green, big things are young. Big things are cold.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The tiger does not see the tiger.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown. I think the correct option is: A\n', '<think>.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "The tiger does not see the tiger" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context. Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the given facts to make it clearer:.\n\n1. The cat chases the dog.\n2. The cat is rough.\n3. The cat is young.\n4. The dog likes the squirrel.\n5. The dog sees the tiger.\n6. The squirrel chases the tiger.\n7. The squirrel is big.\n8. The squirrel is green.\n9. The squirrel likes the dog.\n10. The squirrel sees the dog.\n11. The tiger chases the cat.\n12. The tiger is green.\n13. The tiger likes the dog.\n14. The tiger likes the squirrel.\n15. If something likes the dog, then it chases the tiger.\n16. If something is big and it chases the cat, then the cat likes the squirrel.\n17. If something sees the cat, then the cat is big.\n18. If something likes the squirrel, then it sees the dog.\n19. If something is cold and it chases the cat, then it sees the cat.\n20. If something sees the tiger, then the tiger is big.\n21. Green, big things are young.\n22. Big things are cold.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the tiger does not see the tiger. So, I need to determine if the tiger sees itself or not.\n\nLet me start by looking at the facts about the tiger. From fact 11, the tiger chases the cat. From fact 14, the tiger likes the dog and the squirrel. From fact 12, the tiger is green. From fact 22, big things are cold, so since the tiger is big (from fact 6, the squirrel is big, but I don\'t know if the tiger is big yet), but wait, fact 21 says green, big things are young. So the tiger is green, but I don\'t know if it\'s big yet. Wait, fact 6 says the squirrel is big, but I don\'t see the tiger\'s size directly. Hmm.\n\nWait, let me check fact 20: If something sees the tiger, then the tiger is big. So, if the tiger sees itself, then the tiger would be big. But I don\'t know if the tiger sees itself yet.\n\nAlso, fact 15 says if something likes the dog, then it chases the tiger. The dog likes the squirrel (fact 4), so the dog chases the tiger (fact 5). The squirrel likes the dog (fact 9), so the squirrel sees the dog (fact 10). The squirrel also likes the squirrel? Wait, no, fact 18 says if something likes the squirrel, then it sees the dog. The squirrel likes the dog, so that\'s consistent.\n\nWait, the question is about the tiger seeing the tiger. Let\'s see if there\'s any information about the tiger\'s actions regarding itself.\n\nFrom fact 11, the tiger chases the cat. From fact 17, if something sees the cat, then the cat is big. The cat is young (fact 3), so maybe the cat being big would make it young, but I don\'t know if the cat is big yet.\n\nWait, fact 6 says the squirrel is big, but I don\'t see the cat\'s size. Fact 16 says if something is big and chases the cat, then the cat likes the squirrel. The cat likes the squirrel (fact 4), so maybe the cat is big and chases the squirrel? Wait, no, fact 4 says the dog likes the squirrel, and fact 10 says the squirrel sees the dog. So the squirrel likes the dog, and the dog likes the squirrel. That\'s a mutual liking.\n\nBut I\'m getting a bit confused. Let me try to focus on the tiger.\n\nThe tiger is green (fact 12), likes the dog and squirrel (facts 13 and 14), and from fact 11, it chases the cat. From fact 20, if the tiger sees the tiger, then the tiger is big. So, if the tiger sees itself, it would be big. But does the tiger see itself?.\n\nI don\'t see any information that the tiger sees itself. The facts only mention the tiger seeing the cat (fact 11), the dog (fact 5), and the squirrel (fact 6). Wait, no, fact 5 says the dog sees the tiger, not the tiger seeing the dog. So the tiger chases the cat, likes the dog and squirrel, but I don\'t see it liking itself or seeing itself.\n\nWait, fact 15 says if something likes the dog, it chases the tiger. The dog likes the squirrel, so the dog chases the tiger (fact 5). The squirrel likes the dog, so the squirrel sees the dog (fact 10). The squirrel also likes the squirrel? No, fact 18 says if something likes the squirrel, it sees the dog. The squirrel likes the dog, so that\'s consistent.\n\nBut the question is about the tiger seeing the tiger. Let me see if there\'s any rule that would imply that.\n\nFrom fact 20: If something sees the tiger, then the tiger is big. So, if the tiger sees itself, then the tiger is big. But does the tiger see itself? I don\'t see any information that the tiger sees itself. The facts only mention the tiger seeing the cat, the dog, and the squirrel, but not itself.\n\nWait, fact 17 says if something sees the cat, then the cat is big. The cat is young (fact 3), so if the cat is big, it would still be young. But I don\'t know if the cat is big yet.\n\nWait, fact 6 says the squirrel is big. From fact 21, green, big things are young. The squirrel is green (fact 8) and big, so it\'s young. The squirrel likes the dog (fact 9), and the dog likes the squirrel (fact 4). So the squirrel and dog have a mutual liking.\n\nBut back to the tiger. The tiger is green, likes the dog and squirrel, and chases the cat. If the tiger sees the tiger, it would be big. But is there any evidence that the tiger sees itself?.\n\nI don\'t think so. The facts don\'t mention the tiger seeing itself. So, based on the given information, we can\'t conclude that the tiger sees itself. Therefore, the statement "The tiger does not see the tiger" would be true because there\'s no evidence of the tiger seeing itself.\n\nWait, but the question is whether the statement is true, false, or unknown. Since there\'s no information about the tiger seeing itself, we can\'t say for sure. But wait, the fact 20 says if something sees the tiger, then the tiger is big. So, if the tiger sees itself, it\'s big. But does the tiger see itself? The facts don\'t say, so we can\'t conclude that it does. Therefore, the statement "The tiger does not see the tiger" is unknown because we don\'t have enough information to confirm or deny it.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought that since the tiger is big (from fact 22, big things are cold, and from fact 21, green, big things are young), but I\'m not sure if the tiger is big. Let me check.\n\nFact 22: Big things are cold. Fact 21: Green, big things are young. So, if something is big and green, it\'s young. The squirrel is big and green, so it\'s young. The tiger is green, but I don\'t know if it\'s big yet. From fact 6, the squirrel is big, but the tiger\'s size isn\'t directly given. However, from fact 20, if the tiger sees the tiger, then the tiger is big. But since we don\'t know if the tiger sees itself, we can\'t conclude the tiger\'s size.\n\nWait, but the tiger is green, and from fact 21, green, big things are young. So, if the tiger is big, it\'s young. But we don\'t know if the tiger is big. The facts don\'t specify the tiger\'s size beyond what\'s implied by seeing the cat. From fact 17, if something sees the cat, the cat is big. The cat is young (fact 3), so if the cat is big, it\'s still young. But the cat\'s size isn\'t directly given.\n\nWait, the cat is young (fact 3), but whether it\'s big or not isn\'t specified. So, the cat could be small or big. If the cat is big, then from fact 21, it\'s young. But the cat\'s size isn\'t determined by the facts. So, the tiger\'s size is also unknown.\n\nTherefore, since the tiger\'s size is unknown, we can\'t determine if it\'s big enough to see itself. So, the statement "The tiger does not see the tiger" is unknown because we don\'t have enough information to confirm or deny it.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought that if the tiger sees itself, it\'s big, but since we don\'t know if it sees itself, we can\'t conclude its size. So, the statement is unknown.\n\nWait, but the question is whether the tiger does not see the tiger. If we can\'t determine if it does, then the statement could be either true or unknown. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 56%|#####5    | 334/600 [1:26:12<09:00,  2.03s/it]
