messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nThe cat is big. The cat is cold. The cat likes the cow. The cat visits the lion. The cow is blue. The cow likes the lion. The cow does not visit the lion. The lion chases the cat. The lion is not big. The lion is rough. The lion visits the cat. The squirrel likes the cow. If something visits the squirrel then it likes the cat. If something visits the cat then it likes the cat. If something likes the squirrel and it is big then the squirrel likes the lion. If something likes the cow and it visits the cow then the cow visits the cat. If something likes the cat then it chases the squirrel. If something chases the lion and the lion is not cold then it visits the cow. If something chases the lion then the lion chases the cow. If something chases the squirrel then the squirrel visits the cat. If the cow is rough then the cow chases the cat.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The squirrel does not chase the squirrel.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown. I think the correct option is: C\n', '<think>.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "The squirrel does not chase the squirrel" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context. Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the given statements to make it easier to reference:.\n\n1. The cat is big.\n2. The cat is cold.\n3. The cat likes the cow.\n4. The cat visits the lion.\n5. The cow is blue.\n6. The cow likes the lion.\n7. The cow does not visit the lion.\n8. The lion chases the cat.\n9. The lion is not big.\n10. The lion is rough.\n11. The lion visits the cat.\n12. The squirrel likes the cow.\n13. If something visits the squirrel, then it likes the cat.\n14. If something visits the cat, then it likes the cat.\n15. If something likes the squirrel and it is big, then the squirrel likes the lion.\n16. If something likes the cow and it visits the cow, then the cow visits the cat.\n17. If something likes the cat, then it chases the squirrel.\n18. If something chases the lion and the lion is not cold, then it visits the cow.\n19. If something chases the lion, then the lion chases the cow.\n20. If something chases the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cat.\n21. If the cow is rough, then the cow chases the cat.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the squirrel does not chase the squirrel. So, I need to see if there\'s any information that directly or indirectly tells us about the squirrel\'s actions regarding chasing itself.\n\nLooking through the statements, I don\'t see any direct mention of the squirrel chasing itself. Let\'s see if any of the conditional statements can lead us to that conclusion.\n\nStatement 20 says: If something chases the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the cat. So, if a squirrel (assuming the same entity) chases itself, then the squirrel would visit the cat. But this is a conditional, not a fact. So, we don\'t know for sure if the squirrel actually chases itself.\n\nStatement 13: If something visits the squirrel, then it likes the cat. Again, this is a conditional. It doesn\'t tell us what happens if the squirrel doesn\'t visit itself.\n\nStatement 17: If something likes the cat, then it chases the squirrel. So, if the squirrel likes the cat, it would chase the squirrel. But do we know if the squirrel likes the cat? Statement 12 says the squirrel likes the cow, but there\'s no information about the squirrel liking the cat.\n\nStatement 14: If something visits the cat, then it likes the cat. So, if the squirrel visits the cat, it would like the cat. But we don\'t know if the squirrel visits the cat.\n\nStatement 21: If the cow is rough, then the cow chases the cat. The cow is blue, not rough, so this doesn\'t apply to the squirrel.\n\nLooking at the other animals, the cat and the lion have their own behaviors, but nothing connects the squirrel\'s actions to itself in a way that would allow us to conclude whether it chases itself or not.\n\nTherefore, based on the given information, there\'s no direct or indirect evidence about the squirrel chasing itself. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 57%|#####6    | 342/600 [1:28:15<37:43,  8.78s/it]
