eone eats the dog, they eat the rabbit.\n14. If someone chases the rabbit and the rabbit is green, then they are green.\n15. If someone is green, they eat the dog.\n16. If someone visits the bald eagle and they are round, then the bald eagle is green.\n17. If someone visits the rabbit and they eat the bald eagle, then they eat the dog.\n18. If someone chases the dog, they visit the bald eagle.\n19. If someone is rough and they eat the rabbit, they eat the tiger.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the tiger visits the bald eagle. So, I need to see if there\'s any information that directly or indirectly leads to this conclusion.\n\nLooking at statement 18: "If someone chases the dog, they visit the bald eagle." So, if the tiger chases the dog, then the tiger visits the bald eagle.\n\nFrom statement 8: "The tiger chases the dog." So, the tiger does chase the dog. Therefore, according to statement 18, the tiger must visit the bald eagle.\n\nWait, but let me check if there are any contradictions or missing links. Statement 18 is a conditional, but since the tiger does chase the dog (statement 8), the condition is met, so the conclusion must hold. Therefore, the tiger visits the bald eagle.\n\nI don\'t see any statements that contradict this. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
