 needs the dog, then the dog is round.\n17. If someone visits the dog, then they chase the bald eagle.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the tiger is round. So, I need to see if the given information provides any direct or indirect evidence about the tiger\'s roundness.\n\nLooking at the statements, I see that the tiger\'s roundness isn\'t directly mentioned. However, there are implications through other statements.\n\nLet me see if I can connect the dots. The tiger is kind (statement 10) and not young (statement 11). The bear needs the tiger (statement 5). The bear is not kind (statement 3). The bear needs the bald eagle (statement 4) and the tiger (statement 5). The dog chases both the bald eagle (statement 6) and the bear (statement 7). The dog is not cold (statement 8) and needs the bear (statement 9). .\n\nFrom statement 13: If someone visits the tiger, the tiger does not need the dog. But from statement 17: If someone visits the dog, they chase the bald eagle. And from statement 12: If the bald eagle is visited, it chases the tiger. So, if the bald eagle is visited, it chases the tiger, and if the tiger is visited, it doesn\'t need the dog. But the dog is visiting the bald eagle, so the bald eagle is being chased by someone (maybe the dog). .\n\nWait, the dog is visiting the bald eagle (statement 2), so from statement 12, the bald eagle chases the tiger. So the tiger is being chased by the bald eagle. Now, from statement 15: If someone chases the tiger and the bald eagle, then the bald eagle needs the dog. Since the bald eagle is chasing the tiger, and the bald eagle is being chased by someone (the dog), does that mean the bald eagle needs the dog? So, the bald eagle needs the dog.\n\nFrom statement 16: If someone needs the dog, then the dog is round. So, if the bald eagle needs the dog, then the dog is round. Therefore, the dog is round.\n\nFrom statement 14: If the dog chases the bear and the dog is round, then the bear visits the dog. Since the dog is round (from above), and the dog chases the bear (statement 7), then the bear visits the dog.\n\nNow, the bear needs the tiger (statement 5) and the bald eagle (statement 4). The bear is not kind (statement 3). The tiger is kind (statement 10) and not young (statement 11). .\n\nBut how does this connect to the tiger\'s roundness? The tiger\'s roundness isn\'t mentioned anywhere. The only mention is that the tiger is kind and not young. There\'s no statement that directly or indirectly links the tiger\'s roundness to anything else.\n\nWait, maybe I missed something. Let me check again. The only statements about the tiger are that it\'s kind, not young, and that the bear needs it. There\'s no statement that says the tiger is round or not. So, based on the given information, there\'s no direct or indirect evidence about the tiger\'s roundness. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 62%|######1   | 370/600 [1:36:25<23:35,  6.16s/it]
