, if someone is red, then they are cold. So Erin is red, which means she is cold.\n\nBut wait, the question is about Erin being white. So if Erin is red, she can\'t be white. Unless there\'s some overlap or exception, but I don\'t see any information that allows for someone to be both red and white. Typically, in logic puzzles, unless stated otherwise, things are mutually exclusive.\n\nSo, since Erin is big, she\'s red, and red implies cold. There\'s no information that says someone can be both red and white, so it\'s safe to assume that Erin is red and therefore not white.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. The points about Charlie: Charlie is red and not white (points 7 and 8). So red and not white for Charlie. But does that apply to others? The only other red person mentioned is Charlie, and the rest are big, like Erin and Gary. So if big people are red (point 15), then Erin is red, and thus not white.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Erin is big, so she\'s red, which makes her cold. Since she\'s red, she can\'t be white. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
