g the cow, but does that count as seeing it? The statement says "if something visits the cow and sees the cow," so the bald eagle visiting the cow and seeing the cow would make it visit the bald eagle. But does the bald eagle see the cow? The bald eagle is a predator, so it\'s likely observing the cow, but the statement doesn\'t specify whether seeing is required or just visiting. So, if visiting the cow and seeing the cow is required, then the bald eagle would have to see the cow to visit itself. But the bald eagle is a predator, so it\'s probably not seeing the cow in a friendly manner. Therefore, it\'s possible that the bald eagle visits the cow but doesn\'t see it, so statement 11 doesn\'t apply.\n\nAlternatively, if the bald eagle sees the cow, then it would visit the bald eagle. But since the bald eagle is a predator, it\'s more likely that it\'s observing the cow, not seeing it in a way that would trigger statement 11. Therefore, the bald eagle might not visit itself based on statement 11.\n\nAnother angle: The cat visits the bald eagle (statement 3), and the cat is a predator. The cat is not nice, so according to statement 13, the cat doesn\'t visit the bald eagle because it\'s not nice. But statement 3 says the cat does visit the bald eagle, so there\'s a contradiction here. Maybe the cat is nice, but that\'s not stated. Alternatively, perhaps the cat is not nice, but the statement says that if something visits the bald eagle, then it is nice. So, the cat\'s visit implies it\'s nice, but that\'s not necessarily true. So, perhaps the cat is nice, but that\'s an assumption.\n\nBut regardless, the question is whether the bald eagle visits itself. So, even if the cat is nice, the bald eagle\'s visitation to itself would still depend on whether it\'s nice. Since the bald eagle is not described as nice, it\'s not necessarily nice. Therefore, the bald eagle doesn\'t visit itself.\n\nWait, but statement 13 says if something visits the bald eagle, then it is nice. So, the converse is not necessarily true. Just because something is nice doesn\'t mean it visits the bald eagle. But if something visits the bald eagle, it must be nice. So, the bald eagle itself is not described as visiting itself, so unless it\'s nice, it doesn\'t visit itself. Since the bald eagle is not described as nice, it doesn\'t visit itself.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bald eagle is nice because it\'s blue, but that\'s not stated. The context doesn\'t say that blue makes something nice. So, the bald eagle is not necessarily nice, so it doesn\'t visit itself. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
