 maybe we can find out if he\'s big.\n\nFrom fact 5: All nice things are big. Since Charlie is nice, he must be big. So Charlie is big.\n\nNow, since Charlie is big and nice, from fact 12, he must be quiet. So Charlie is quiet.\n\nFrom fact 7: Blue, furry things are quiet. But we don\'t know if Charlie is blue or furry yet.\n\nFrom fact 6: All furry things are blue. So if Charlie is furry, he\'s blue. But we don\'t know if he\'s furry.\n\nWait, let\'s see if we can find out if Charlie is furry. From fact 10: Big, quiet things are furry. Since Charlie is big and quiet, he must be furry.\n\nSo Charlie is big, quiet, and furry. From fact 6, being furry means he\'s blue. So Charlie is blue and furry.\n\nFrom fact 11: If something is blue and furry, then it\'s smart. Since Charlie is blue and furry, he must be smart.\n\nWait, but the question is whether Charlie is not smart. So if Charlie is smart, then the statement "Charlie is not smart" would be false.\n\nBut let me double-check. Charlie is nice (given), so from fact 5, he\'s big. Then from fact 12, since he\'s nice and big, he\'s quiet. Then from fact 10, big and quiet things are furry, so he\'s furry. From fact 6, being furry means he\'s blue. From fact 11, being blue and furry means he\'s smart. So yes, Charlie is smart. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
