 is nice and round, then they are rough. So, in logical terms, that\'s (Nice ∧ Round) → Rough. The contrapositive would be ¬Rough → ¬(Nice ∧ Round), which is the same as ¬Rough → ¬Nice ∨ ¬Round. But I\'m not sure if that helps directly.\n\nStatement 12 says Nice people are round. So, if someone is nice, then they are round. That\'s a direct implication: Nice → Round.\n\nWait, but statement 4 says Fiona is nice. So according to statement 12, if she\'s nice, then she must be round. So that would mean Fiona is round.\n\nBut the question is asking if Fiona is not round. If she is round, then the statement "Fiona is not round" would be false. So, based on that, the answer would be B) False.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Statement 12 says Nice people are round. So if Fiona is nice, she must be round. Therefore, she is round, making the statement "Fiona is not round" false.\n\nI don\'t see any contradictions or other statements that would change this conclusion. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
