It\'s more about the contrapositive, but since Bob is white, the condition doesn\'t apply.\n\nPoint 10 directly links being white to being quiet. So, Bob being white should make him quiet. But wait, let me check if there\'s any other information that might contradict this.\n\nPoint 11 says if someone is nice and quiet, then they are blue. Bob is nice (point 3) and if he\'s quiet (which we\'re considering), then he should be blue. But from point 6, Gary is blue, and point 12 says all white, furry people are blue. Bob is white, so if he\'s furry, he\'s blue. But we don\'t know if Bob is furry.\n\nWait, but from point 12, if Bob is white and furry, he\'s blue. But we don\'t know if he\'s furry. So, Bob could be white and not furry, which would mean he\'s white and not necessarily blue. But from point 10, being white makes him quiet, regardless of his furiness.\n\nSo, the key point is that Bob is white, and being white implies he\'s quiet. Therefore, the statement "Bob is quiet" should be true.\n\nI don\'t see any contradictions or missing information that would make this statement false. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
