on. But perhaps the context is designed to have this contradiction, and we have to proceed.\n\nWait, but the question is about whether Gary is not round. If Gary is round, then the statement "Gary is not round" would be false. But if Gary is not round, then the statement would be true. But from the reasoning, if Gary is blue, he must be young (from point 10). Then, since he\'s young and white, he must be round (from point 12). So Gary must be round. Therefore, the statement "Gary is not round" would be false.\n\nBut wait, point 4 says Gary is white, and point 3 says he\'s blue. That\'s a contradiction, but perhaps we have to ignore that for the sake of the problem. Maybe the context is designed to have this contradiction, but the question is about whether Gary is not round, regardless of the color.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the color is irrelevant to the question about being round. So, from point 12, if Gary is young and white, he\'s round. But we don\'t know if Gary is white. Wait, point 4 says he\'s white, so he must be. Therefore, he must be round. So the statement "Gary is not round" would be false.\n\nBut wait, point 4 says Gary is white, and point 3 says he\'s blue. That\'s a problem because he can\'t be both. So perhaps the context is inconsistent, but we have to proceed. Maybe the answer is that Gary is round because he\'s young and white, so the statement "Gary is not round" is false.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the color information is a red herring, and the key is point 12: if something is young and white, it\'s round. So regardless of the color, if Gary is young and white, he\'s round. But we don\'t know if Gary is white. Wait, point 4 says he\'s white, so he must be. Therefore, he\'s round. So the statement "Gary is not round" is false.\n\nBut wait, point 4 says Gary is white, and point 3 says he\'s blue. That\'s a contradiction, but perhaps we have to assume that the context is correct, and maybe the colors are not necessarily both. Maybe the context is tryi