the rabbit, but I don\'t have information about the lion\'s size.\n\nWait, statement 8 says if something chases the rabbit, then it is big. So if the lion chases the rabbit, then the lion is big. But does the lion chase the rabbit? Statement 3 says the lion needs the rabbit, but that doesn\'t necessarily mean it chases it. However, statement 14 says if the lion chases the lion, then it chases the rabbit. But we don\'t know if the lion chases itself.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the lion doesn\'t need to chase itself because it\'s already big enough, but I don\'t have information about the lion\'s size.\n\nWait, maybe the lion is big because it needs the lion (statement 6), but that\'s not necessarily about size. Statement 9 says if something needs the tiger, then it\'s big. But the lion doesn\'t need the tiger, so that doesn\'t apply.\n\nI\'m getting stuck here. Let me try to see if there\'s any way to conclude whether the lion chases itself.\n\nIf the lion were to chase itself, then according to statement 14, the lion would chase the rabbit. But does the lion chase itself? There\'s no direct information about that. The statements don\'t mention the lion chasing itself or any other animal chasing itself.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the lion doesn\'t need to chase itself because it\'s already big enough, but again, there\'s no information about the lion\'s size or whether it\'s big.\n\nWait, statement 8 says if something chases the rabbit, then it\'s big. So if the lion chases the rabbit, it\'s big. But does the lion chase the rabbit? Statement 3 says the lion needs the rabbit, but that doesn\'t necessarily mean it chases it. However, statement 14 says if the lion chases the lion, then it chases the rabbit. But we don\'t know if the lion chases itself.\n\nI think the key is that the lion needs the rabbit (statement 3), and the rabbit needs the lion (statement 6). So they have a mutual dependency. But does that imply that the lion chases itself? Not necessarily. The statements