blue things are red, so Charlie would be red. But from rule 12, red things are blue, so he\'s both red and blue, which is possible.\n\nBut if Charlie isn\'t cold, then rule 10 doesn\'t apply, so he doesn\'t have to be rough. But rule 14 says if he\'s round and cold, he\'s blue, which he already is. So being cold isn\'t required for him to be blue.\n\nSo, can Charlie be red? If he is red, then from rule 12, he\'s blue, which he already is. But from rule 11, if he\'s red and blue, he\'s rough. So being red would make him rough. But from rule 14, if he\'s round and cold, he\'s blue. So being red and blue would require him to be rough, but that doesn\'t conflict with anything else.\n\nWait, but if Charlie is red, then he\'s blue (from rule 12), and if he\'s blue and red, he\'s rough (from rule 11). But from rule 14, being round and cold makes him blue, but if he\'s red, he\'s also blue, so he could be both. But does that mean he\'s cold? Because if he\'s red, he\'s blue, but rule 14 only applies if he\'s round and cold. So being red doesn\'t necessarily make him cold, unless he\'s round and cold, which he is, but being red doesn\'t require him to be cold.\n\nWait, no. Rule 14 says if something is round and cold, then it\'s blue. So being round and cold makes it blue, but being blue doesn\'t necessarily make it round and cold. So Charlie is blue and round, but we don\'t know if he\'s cold. If he\'s cold, then he\'s blue and round, making him rough (from rule 8). But if he\'s not cold, then he\'s blue and round, but not necessarily rough.\n\nBut if he\'s red, then from rule 12, he\'s blue, and from rule 11, he\'s rough. So being red would make him rough, but he\'s already round. So being red would require him to be rough, but he\'s already round. So is there a conflict?.\n\nWait, let\'s think again. If Charlie is red, then he\'s blue (rule 12). If he\'s blue and red, then he\'s rough (rule 11). So he\'s rough. But from rule 14, if he\'s round and cold, he\'s blue. So bein