ot see the bald eagle.\n\nFrom statement 8: If someone needs the mouse, then the mouse does not see the bald eagle. But from statement 12, we know that someone does not see the bald eagle because they need the lion. So, does that mean someone needs the mouse? Not necessarily, because the condition in statement 8 is about needing the mouse leading to not seeing the bald eagle. But we know that someone does not see the bald eagle, but that doesn\'t necessarily mean that someone needs the mouse. It could be someone else.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can link the mouse seeing the bald eagle. From statement 8, if someone needs the mouse, then the mouse doesn\'t see the bald eagle. But we don\'t know if the mouse is seeing the bald eagle or not.\n\nWait, from statement 11: If someone sees the mouse, then the mouse is kind. So, seeing the mouse implies the mouse is kind. But we don\'t know if the mouse is seeing someone or not.\n\nAlso, from statement 15: If someone sees the mouse, then they like the lion. So, seeing the mouse leads to liking the lion.\n\nBut we don\'t have any information about the mouse liking the lion or not. So, maybe the mouse is kind because someone sees it, but we don\'t know if someone is seeing it.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse is kind because it likes the dog (statement 6), but that doesn\'t necessarily make it rough.\n\nWait, statement 14: All kind people are rough. So, if someone is kind, they are rough. But that doesn\'t say anything about non-kind people; they might or might not be rough.\n\nSo, if the mouse is kind, then it\'s rough. But do we know if the mouse is kind?.\n\nFrom statement 11: If someone sees the mouse, then the mouse is kind. So, seeing the mouse makes it kind. But we don\'t know if someone is seeing the mouse.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse is kind because it likes the dog (statement 6), but that doesn\'t necessarily mean someone sees it.\n\nWait, but the mouse likes the dog (statement 6), and the dog needs the lion (s