don\'t know if he\'s cold. Statement 15 says cold things are rough, and statement 14 says rough, nice things are round. So, if something is cold, it\'s rough, and if it\'s rough and nice, it\'s round. But Dave is nice, but we don\'t know if he\'s cold or not.\n\nWait, statement 16 says that if something is red and nice, then it is cold. Dave is red and nice, so according to statement 16, he must be cold. So Dave is cold.\n\nNow, statement 15 says cold things are rough. So if Dave is cold, he\'s rough. Statement 14 says rough, nice things are round. So Dave, being rough and nice, must be round.\n\nBut statement 17 says all round things are green. So if Dave is round, he\'s green. However, statement 13 says red things are nice, and statement 12 says smart, round things are nice. But we don\'t have any information about Dave\'s smartness directly.\n\nWait, statement 12: If something is smart and round, then it\'s nice. But we know Dave is nice and round (from above). So does that mean he\'s smart? Not necessarily. Because the statement is an implication: if smart and round, then nice. But Dave is nice and round, so it doesn\'t necessarily mean he\'s smart. It could be that he\'s smart, but it\'s not required. So we can\'t conclude he\'s smart based on the given information.\n\nAdditionally, statement 11 says Gary is not smart. But Gary is a different entity from Dave, so there\'s no information linking Dave\'s smartness to Gary\'s. So we can\'t infer anything about Dave\'s smartness from Gary\'s lack of smartness.\n\nTherefore, based on the given statements, we don\'t have enough information to determine whether Dave is smart or not. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
