the lion (statement 4). Is that possible? The cat can chase both the lion and the cow, and eat the bald eagle. There\'s no direct contradiction, as long as the cat isn\'t required to do only one thing.\n\nBut let me check if the cat chasing the cow leads to any other conclusions. From statement 14: If something chases the cow and the cow chases the cat, then it is rough. So, if the cat chases the cow, and the cow chases the cat, then the cat is rough. But the cat is already rough (statement 6), so that\'s consistent.\n\nWait, but does the cow chase the cat? From statement 16: If something chases the cat, then it sees the bald eagle. The cat is chasing the cat? That doesn\'t make sense. Alternatively, perhaps the cat is chasing the cat, but that\'s not logical. Maybe I\'m misapplying the statements.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the cat is chasing the cat, but that\'s not possible. So, maybe the cat is chasing the cat, which would mean it\'s chasing itself, which is impossible. Therefore, perhaps the cat cannot be chasing the cow because that would require the cat to chase itself, which is impossible.\n\nWait, that might be a key point. If the cat chases the cow, then from statement 16: If something chases the cat, then it sees the bald eagle. But the cat is chasing the cow, not the cat. So, statement 16 doesn\'t apply here. Therefore, the cat can chase the cow without any issues.\n\nBut then, from statement 14: If something chases the cow and the cow chases the cat, then it is rough. So, if the cat chases the cow, and the cow chases the cat, then the cat is rough. But the cat is already rough, so that\'s consistent.\n\nBut does the cow chase the cat? From statement 16: If something chases the cat, then it sees the bald eagle. So, if the cat is chasing the cat, then it sees the bald eagle. But the cat can\'t chase itself, so that scenario is impossible. Therefore, the condition in statement 14 is never met because the cow can\'t chase the cat if the cat is chasing the co