r\'s eating the bald eagle is separate.\n\nWait, but statement 19 says if the tiger eats the bald eagle, then the tiger does not eat the mouse. So, if tiger eats → bald eagle, then tiger does not eat → mouse.\n\nBut the bear is eating the bald eagle, so the tiger might or might not eat the bald eagle. Let\'s assume the tiger doesn\'t eat the bald eagle because the bear is eating it, but that\'s not necessarily stated.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger does eat the bald eagle, but then from statement 19, the tiger would not eat the mouse.\n\nBut I\'m not sure if the tiger eats the bald eagle or not. Let\'s see.\n\nFrom statement 4, the bald eagle does not like the bear. So, bald eagle → does not like → bear.\n\nBut the bear eats the bald eagle, so the bear is eating something it doesn\'t like. That\'s possible, but not necessarily relevant.\n\nNow, looking at statement 14: If something eats the bald eagle and the bald eagle likes the tiger, then the tiger likes the mouse.\n\nSo, if something → eats → bald eagle, and bald eagle → likes → tiger, then tiger → likes → mouse.\n\nBut from statement 4, the bald eagle does not like the bear, so the bear is eating the bald eagle, but the bald eagle doesn\'t like the bear. So, the condition for statement 14 is: something eats the bald eagle (which is the bear) and the bald eagle likes the tiger.\n\nWait, the bald eagle does not like the tiger. From statement 4, the bald eagle does not like the bear, but statement 4 says the bald eagle does not like the bear. Wait, no, statement 4 is "The bald eagle does not like the bear." So, the bald eagle doesn\'t like the bear, but statement 12 says the tiger likes the bear. So, the bald eagle doesn\'t like the bear, but the tiger does.\n\nSo, the condition in statement 14 is: something eats the bald eagle (bear) and the bald eagle likes the tiger. But the bald eagle doesn\'t like the tiger. So, the condition is not met. Therefore, statement 14 doesn\'t apply here.\n\nSo, the tiger liking 