er.\n\nWait, that\'s a bit circular. Let me try to break it down.\n\nIf the bear likes the tiger (bear → likes → tiger), then from statement 17, if the bear visits the bear (which it does) and is kind, then the bear likes the tiger. But that\'s a bit redundant because the bear is already liking the tiger.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bear visiting the mouse would make it kind, but I\'m not sure.\n\nWait, statement 20: If something visits the mouse, then it is kind. So, if the bear visits the mouse, then the bear is kind.\n\nBut from statement 15, if something likes the tiger, it visits the mouse. So, if the bear likes the tiger, it would visit the mouse, making the bear kind.\n\nBut does the bear like the tiger? From statement 12, the tiger likes the bear, but that doesn\'t mean the bear likes the tiger. So, unless there\'s a mutual liking, we can\'t assume that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bear likes the mouse because the mouse likes the tiger, but that\'s not directly connected.\n\nWait, let\'s think about statement 19: If the tiger eats the bald eagle, then the tiger does not eat the mouse. But the bear is eating the bald eagle, not the tiger. So, the tiger might or might not eat the bald eagle. If the tiger does eat the bald eagle, then it can\'t eat the mouse. If it doesn\'t, then it might eat the mouse.\n\nBut we don\'t know if the tiger eats the bald eagle or not. So, that\'s uncertain.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s look at statement 14 again: If something eats the bald eagle and the bald eagle likes the tiger, then the tiger likes the mouse.\n\nSo, if the bear eats the bald eagle (which it does), and the bald eagle likes the tiger (which it doesn\'t, from statement 4), then the condition isn\'t met. So, statement 14 doesn\'t apply here.\n\nSo, the tiger liking the mouse isn\'t triggered by the bear eating the bald eagle.\n\nNow, let\'s see if the mouse being big affects anything. From statement 16: If the tiger likes the mouse and the mouse does not eat the tiger,