l quiet, kind things are white. Since Bob is quiet, if he\'s kind, he must be white, which he is. But that doesn\'t tell us if he\'s kind or not.\n\nPoint 17 says all young, quiet things are kind. So, if something is young, quiet, it\'s kind. But Bob is quiet, but we don\'t know if he\'s young. Point 19 says if Gary is young, then Gary is round. But Gary is red and round, so if Gary is young, that\'s consistent. However, Bob\'s age isn\'t mentioned, so we don\'t know if he\'s young or not.\n\nPoint 14 says all round things are young. So, if something is round, it\'s young. But Bob is white and round (from point 11). So, since Bob is round, he must be young. Wait, no, point 11 says white things are round. So, Bob is white, so he\'s round. Then, point 14 says all round things are young, so Bob must be young.\n\nSo, Bob is quiet, white, and young. Now, point 17 says all young, quiet things are kind. So, Bob is kind. Then, point 12 says if something is kind and white, it\'s green. Bob is kind and white, so he should be green.\n\nBut wait, point 16 says quiet, green things are red. So, if Bob is quiet and green, he should be red. But Bob is white, not green. That seems contradictory. Let me check again.\n\nWait, point 12: If something is kind and white, then it\'s green. So, Bob is kind and white, so he must be green. But point 16 says quiet, green things are red. So, if Bob is quiet and green, he should be red. But Bob is white, not green. So, there\'s a contradiction here.\n\nHmm, maybe I made a mistake. Let me go through it again.\n\nBob is quiet (1), white (2). So, he\'s quiet and white. From point 11, white things are round, so Bob is round. From point 14, all round things are young, so Bob is young. From point 17, all young, quiet things are kind, so Bob is kind. From point 12, if something is kind and white, it\'s green. So, Bob is kind and white, so he\'s green. But point 16 says quiet, green things are red. So, if Bob is quiet and green, he should be red. But Bob is white, not green. That\'s a problem.\n\nWait, maybe I misapplied the rules. Let\'s see:.\n\n- Bob is quiet and white, so he\'s round (point 11).\n- Being round means he\'s young (point 14).\n- Being young and quiet means he\'s kind (point 17).\n- Being kind and white means he\'s green (point 12).\n- Being quiet and green means he\'s red (point 16).\n\nSo, according to this, Bob should be red. But Bob is white. That\'s a contradiction. So, is Bob red or not?.\n\nWait, maybe the rules are such that if Bob is white, he can\'t be green, so the chain breaks. Let me think.\n\nIf Bob is white, then from point 12, being kind and white would make him green. But he\'s white, so he can\'t be green. Therefore, he can\'t be kind? But from point 17, being young and quiet makes him kind. Bob is young and quiet, so he must be kind. But if he\'s kind and white, he must be green, which contradicts him being white.\n\nThis seems like a paradox. Maybe the rules are such that if Bob is white, he can\'t be kind, but he\'s young and quiet, so he must be kind. Therefore, the only way to resolve this is if Bob is not red, because he\'s white, but the rules suggest he should be red.\n\nWait, but the question is whether Bob is not red. So, if Bob is white, he\'s not red, right? But according to the rules, he should be red. So, is the statement "Bob is not red" true or false?.\n\nWait, if Bob is white, then he\'s not red, so the statement "Bob is not red" would be true. But according to the rules, he should be red, which would make the statement false. But that\'s conflicting.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rules don\'t apply to Bob because he\'s white, so he can\'t be kind, but he\'s young and quiet, so he must be kind. That\'s a contradiction. So, perhaps the statement "Bob is not red" is true because he\'s white, but the rules suggest he should be red, making the statement false. But that\'s confusing.\n\nWait, maybe the rules are such that if Bob is white, he can\'t be kind, but he\'s yo